const QUESTIONS = [{"n":1,"topic":2,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image1\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou need to implement agent2 to meet the technical requirements.<br />\nHow should you complete the YAML configuration? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image2\" src=\"images/image2.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image3.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A custom agent (a <b>.agent.md</b> file) is a saved persona for Copilot: its <b>tools</b> list decides what it may do. <b>search</b> finds files and code, <b>read</b> opens and inspects them, <b>edit</b> changes files, <b>execute</b> runs shell commands, <b>agent</b> delegates to subagents, <b>web</b> browses the internet. A planning-only agent must look but never touch.</div><div class=\"why\">Box 1 = <b>search</b>, Box 2 = <b>read</b>: everything a plan needs, nothing it must not do.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>edit</b> would let the planner rewrite files.</li><li><b>execute</b> would let it run local commands.</li><li><b>agent</b> delegation is unnecessary for writing a plan.</li><li><b>web</b> is redundant: Microsoft Learn access already comes from the listed microsoftdocs MCP tools.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> A custom agent is a saved persona for Copilot kept in a <b>.agent.md</b> file: a name, a description, a <b>tools</b> allow-list and instructions. The tools list is a permission boundary, exactly like giving a coworker a badge that opens only some doors. <b>search</b> finds files and code ('where is login handled?'), <b>read</b> opens them, <b>edit</b> rewrites them, <b>execute</b> runs shell commands, <b>agent</b> lets it delegate to subagents, <b>web</b> browses the internet, and entries like <b>microsoftdocs/mcp/docs_search</b> are Model Context Protocol tools that query Microsoft Learn docs. A planning-only agent such as implementation-planner must look but never touch, so it gets only search and read. <b>Example:</b> you ask it to plan API rate limiting. It searches for the auth code, reads the controllers and the Learn docs on throttling, then writes a step-by-step plan. With edit or execute it could rewrite your controllers unasked; with web it could leak internal code into public search queries. That is why plan-only means search plus read, nothing more.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (tools, handoffs)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":2,"topic":2,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image4\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nBefore App1 is upgraded, you need to verify each individual upgrade step and whether all tests have passed.<br />\nWhich file should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tassessment.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tplan.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tagent.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ttasks.md<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The Copilot app-modernization agent upgrades legacy apps in three stages. <b>assessment.md</b> is the analysis report (breaking changes), <b>plan.md</b> is the strategy, and <b>tasks.md</b> is the live dashboard where every step flips through start, execute, validate (build + test), complete.</div><div class=\"why\">To verify each step and test results, read the live dashboard: <b>tasks.md</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A assessment.md</b> is written before the upgrade: analysis only, no step status.</li><li><b>B plan.md</b> describes strategy; it never tracks execution.</li><li><b>C agent.md</b> is not a modernization artifact at all.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot app-modernization agent upgrades legacy applications in three visible stages, and each stage writes its own file under <b>.github/upgrades/{scenario}</b>. <b>Stage 1 Assessment</b> scans structure, dependencies and code patterns and writes <b>assessment.md</b>: breaking changes, deprecated APIs, upgrade scope. You review it, confirm strategy decisions (saved to upgrade-options.md), then <b>Stage 2 Planning</b> writes <b>plan.md</b> (the strategy in prose) plus <b>tasks.md</b> (the ordered checklist). <b>Stage 3 Execution</b> works the checklist top to bottom; every task cycles start, execute, validate (build and test), complete, and the agent rewrites tasks.md with live status markers as it goes. <b>Example:</b> upgrading App1 from .NET 6 to .NET 10, tasks.md shows TASK-001 project files [x] done, TASK-002 EF6 to EF Core [.] in progress, TASK-003 full test suite [ ] pending. Opening tasks.md answers 'which step are we on and did tests pass' at a glance. assessment.md cannot answer that: it was frozen before execution began.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-app-modernization/concepts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App modernization: assessment, plan, tasks.md</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-upgrade/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot upgrade: how the stages work</a></li></ul>"},{"n":3,"topic":2,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that has a GitHub Actions workflow. The workflow runs an AI agent.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the default GITHUB_TOKEN permissions are read-only, and write access is granted to only the job that performs repository write operations. The workflow must be able to create and approve pull requests only when explicitly enabled.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image5\" src=\"images/image4.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image5.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Every Actions workflow gets a <b>GITHUB_TOKEN</b> proving its identity. <b>permissions</b> blocks decide what that token may touch. <b>contents</b> covers repo files, <b>pull-requests</b> covers PR operations, <b>issues</b> covers issues. Least privilege = default read-only, grant write only where needed.</div><div class=\"why\">Top-level <b>contents: read</b> (read-only default) · job <b>contents: write</b> (only the writer job) · step <b>pull-requests: write</b> (explicit PR create/approve).</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>permissions: read-all</b> is not the required default here, and read-only never grants write.</li><li><b>issues: write</b>: nothing in the workflow touches issues.</li><li><b>pull-requests: read</b> cannot create or approve PRs.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Every GitHub Actions run receives a <b>GITHUB_TOKEN</b>: a short-lived credential proving 'I am this workflow'. A <b>permissions</b> block scopes what that token may touch, and scopes are fine-grained: <b>contents</b> covers files and commits, <b>pull-requests</b> covers opening, commenting and approving PRs, <b>issues</b> covers issues. Permissions can be set once at workflow top (the default for all jobs) and tightened or widened per job. <b>Least privilege</b> means default read-only and grant write only to the job that must write. <b>Example:</b> the agent-pr workflow has an analyze job (reads PR content, posts a comment) and an update_artifacts job (regenerates files, creates a PR). Top-level contents: read keeps analyze honest; contents: write on update_artifacts lets it commit; pull-requests: write on the create step lets it open the PR. If the default were write, any compromised step, or any agent prompt-injection hidden in PR text, could push code or approve merges. Read-by-default shrinks that blast radius to zero.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-guides/automatic-token-authentication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GITHUB_TOKEN permissions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":4,"topic":2,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues and create draft pull requests. The repository uses GitHub Actions for CI, and reviewers rely on pull request timelines and workflow artifacts to understand what the agent did.<br />\nDuring long-running agent tasks, the reviewers lose track of decisions and validation steps, which causes repeated questions and reworks when context drifts between iterations.<br />\nYou need to persist task progress and decisions as durable artifacts and ensure that the reviewers can verify what the agent did during and after execution by using GitHub as the system of record.<br />\nWhat should you do for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image7\" src=\"images/image6.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image7.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Reviewers cannot see inside an agent run, so GitHub is the system of record: <b>artifacts</b> (upload-artifact) persist files after a run, the <b>PR body + timeline</b> narrate progress on the PR itself, and <b>View session</b> streams the live log.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>upload-artifact + retention</b> = durable downloadable outputs · <b>PR body/timeline</b> = reviewable signals · <b>View session</b> = real time.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Assigning an issue starts work but records nothing.</li><li>Approve-and-run is a manual gate, not tracking.</li><li>Mentioning @copilot on a merged PR restarts the agent instead of tracking it.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> You cannot watch an agent the way you watch a colleague, so GitHub gives reviewers three lenses with different time horizons. <b>Artifacts</b> (actions/upload-artifact, with a retention-days setting) persist files after the run ends: durable evidence you can download next week. The <b>PR body and timeline</b> are the running narrative on the pull request itself: the agent edits the PR description with decisions and every event lands in the timeline. <b>View session</b> streams the live log while the agent works. <b>Example:</b> Monday morning you open an agent PR from Friday. The timeline tells you it tried three approaches and picked one; you download plan.md from artifacts to read the reasoning; and when you ask for a revision, View session lets you watch it work in real time. Assigning issues starts work, approving workflows gates it, mentioning @copilot steers it: none of those records what happened, which is why they answer different questions.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/storing-workflow-data-as-artifacts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow artifacts</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":5,"topic":2,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues asynchronously.<br />\n<br />\nWhen an issue is assigned to GitHub Copilot, the agent creates a draft pull request, but your team cannot always tell whether the agent is actively working, has completed its session, or is awaiting workflow approval.<br />\nWhich execution context does each signal indicate? To answer, drag the appropriate context to the correct signals. Each signal may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image9\" src=\"images/image8.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<img src=\"images/image9.png\" alt=\"official answer\"><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> When you assign an issue to Copilot, it reacts and works: an <b>eyes reaction</b> means it accepted, <b>timeline updates</b> narrate the run, and if <b>Actions checks sit idle</b>, the repo likely requires manual workflow approval (common for agent PRs).</div><div class=\"why\">Eyes = <b>acknowledged, PR coming</b> · timeline started = <b>actively running</b> · checks idle = <b>human must approve and run workflows</b>.</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Assigning an issue to Copilot starts a full lifecycle. First the agent <b>acknowledges</b>: GitHub shows an eyes reaction on the issue meaning 'accepted, I am on it'. Then it opens a branch, commits work in progress and pushes, and the <b>PR timeline</b> narrates each phase with status updates. Finally it opens a draft PR and CI runs. Many repos require a human to <b>Approve and run</b> workflows on agent PRs (a fork-safety habit), so checks can sit idle waiting for you: that is normal, not broken. <b>Example timeline reading:</b> eyes reaction, no branch after 10 minutes means the agent never started (check assignment and permissions). Branch plus commits plus a 55-minute-old status means it stalled mid-run (nudge it). Draft PR with grey idle checks means go click Approve and run. Each signal maps to exactly one next action.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":6,"topic":2,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent and supports hooks stored under .github/hooks.<br />\nYou need a Shell command to run automatically whenever an agent execution fails.<br />\nWhich type of hook should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tpostToolUse<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tsessionEnd<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\terrorOccurred<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tagentStop<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The Copilot CLI supports <b>hooks</b>: small shell scripts fired on lifecycle events. <b>postToolUse</b> runs after each tool call, <b>sessionEnd</b> at session end, <b>errorOccurred</b> when an execution fails.</div><div class=\"why\">A command on every agent failure needs the failure event: <b>errorOccurred</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>postToolUse</b> fires after every tool call, failures or not.</li><li><b>sessionEnd</b> fires once at the end regardless of outcome.</li><li><b>agentStop</b> is about the agent stopping, not about an execution error.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot CLI can run <b>hooks</b>: shell scripts stored under .github/hooks that fire on lifecycle events. Think of them as tripwires. <b>preToolUse</b> fires before a tool runs and can veto it. <b>postToolUse</b> fires after a tool completes, good for logging or follow-up checks. <b>userPromptSubmitted</b> fires when you send a prompt. <b>sessionStart / sessionEnd</b> fire once per session. <b>errorOccurred</b> fires when an agent execution fails. <b>Example:</b> a team pages on-call whenever nightly agent runs fail: an errorOccurred hook running a curl to the paging API fires only on failure. A postToolUse hook would page on every tool call, and sessionEnd would page even on success. Matching the hook to the event is the whole skill: before versus after, every-call versus on-failure.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":7,"topic":2,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You are about to start a complex refactoring task in the GitHub Copilot CLI.<br />\nBefore Copilot makes any changes, you need to review and agree on the approach.<br />\nWhat should you do first?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the Copilot CLI and specify the --agent=Task parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the Copilot CLI and specify the --allow-all parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tFrom the Copilot CLI, switch to plan mode.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tFrom the Copilot CLI, run the /compact command.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Plan mode</b> is a CLI working style: Copilot investigates, writes up its proposed approach, and waits for your approval before changing a single file. Flags like <b>--allow-all</b> do the opposite (skip all approvals); <b>/compact</b> only shrinks conversation context.</div><div class=\"why\">Review-then-agree before any change = <b>plan mode</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A --agent=Task</b> only picks an agent; it adds no approval gate.</li><li><b>B --allow-all</b> removes approvals entirely.</li><li><b>D /compact</b> manages context size; it produces no approvable plan.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot CLI has working styles with opposite philosophies. Normal/agent mode acts immediately. <b>Plan mode</b> investigates first: it reads the repo, researches, then presents a written approach and waits. Nothing changes until you approve, at which point it implements the agreed plan. Flags modify autonomy: <b>--allow-all</b> pre-approves every tool (maximum speed, minimum safety), and slash commands do chores: <b>/compact</b> shrinks context, <b>/usage</b> shows tokens. <b>Example:</b> asked to refactor authentication, plan mode returns 'I will extract a handler, update 4 controllers, add tests' and pauses. You spot it missed the mobile client, correct the plan, approve, and only then does code change. Starting with --agent=Task merely picks a persona, and --allow-all skips the very review the requirement demands.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":8,"topic":2,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  A team assigns an issue to the GitHub Copilot coding agent by using the following one-line description: Fix the login bug.<br />\nCopilot creates a pull request, but the pull request is missing changes and has an incorrect scope.<br />\nHow should you resolve the issue?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable Copilot memory.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate the Model Context Protocol (MCP) server rate limits.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd a clear description of the problem to the issue.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAllocate more resources to copilot-setup-steps.yml.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The coding agent plans from the issue text: a one-liner with no scope, no expected changes and no acceptance criteria leaves it guessing, so the PR comes back thin. Rich issue context is the cheapest quality lever.</div><div class=\"why\">The one-liner hid scope and expected changes, so the agent planned from almost nothing. A <b>clear problem description</b> repairs the actual input the agent reasons from.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A memory</b> personalizes style; it cannot invent missing scope.</li><li><b>B MCP rate limits</b> throttle doc retrieval; unrelated to PR scope.</li><li><b>D setup-steps resources</b> fix the runner environment, not issue quality.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The coding agent plans from the issue text, so issue quality caps result quality. A one-liner like 'fix login' hides scope (which login: web, mobile, SSO?), expected behavior, reproduction steps and acceptance criteria, and the agent fills the gaps by guessing. A good issue states problem, context, desired outcome and how to verify. Nothing else in the option list feeds the agent better input: memory tunes style, MCP limits tune doc retrieval, runner resources tune speed. <b>Example:</b> 'Users report login fails' produces a PR touching the wrong login form. 'On the web React form, SSO login loops after password reset; reproduce with a federated test account; done means the loop is gone and existing tests pass' produces a scoped, verifiable PR. When reviews complain an agent PR 'missed the point', rewrite the issue first.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":9,"topic":3,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image11\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to configure agent1 to support the planned changes.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tDelete line 05 from the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd Use all available tools to the .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd Use all available tools to the instructions in the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the mcp-servers property to the agent configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the agent configuration, replace line 05 with the following.05 tools: [].<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Agent1 profile line 05 pins <b>tools: [read,search,edit]</b>. In agent profiles, omitting the tools key restores the default: everything available. MCP1, meanwhile, is wired through the repository MCP configuration (planned change 3), not the agent profile.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Delete line 05</b>: the restriction itself is what blocks full tool access.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B</b> prose in copilot-instructions.md also leaks into Chat and review (forbidden by change 2), and prose grants no tool access.</li><li><b>C</b> prose inside the profile likewise grants nothing.</li><li><b>D mcp-servers</b> belongs in the repo MCP config per the planned change, not the agent profile.</li><li><b>E tools: []</b> is an empty allow-list: the exact opposite of all-access.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> An agent profile (the YAML frontmatter of a .agent.md file) has two halves that beginners confuse. The <b>tools</b> key is a machine-enforced allow-list: only listed tools can run. The <b>instructions</b> prose is guidance the model reads: influential but not enforced. 'Use all available tools' written in prose grants nothing. Separately, <b>MCP servers</b> (external tool providers like MCP1) are wired through repository MCP configuration files, not through the agent profile. <b>Example:</b> agent1 shows tools: [read,search,edit]. The planned change 'access all tools' is achieved by deleting that line so the default (everything) applies. MCP1 access comes from the repo mcp.json change, a different planned change. Adding an mcp-servers block to the profile answers the wrong change, and tools: [] would lock the agent out of everything.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (tools, handoffs)</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":10,"topic":3,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image12\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to provide access to the API key of MCP1. The solution must meet the security requirements.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the API key as a GitHub Codespaces user secret scoped to product-api.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the API key as a secret in the Copilot environment of product-api by using a name prefix of COPILOT_MCP_, and then reference the variable name in the mcp.json configuration.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the product-api repository settings, add the API key directly to the .mcp/server.json file by using a plaintext apiKey field.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn product-api, add the API key as a GitHub Actions encrypted secret and reference the secret by using ${{ secrets.KEY }} in the workflow YAML of agent1.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The coding agent cannot see normal Actions secrets, and anything committed to git is visible to every reader. MCP credentials therefore live as <b>COPILOT_MCP_-prefixed secrets in the Copilot environment</b>, referenced by variable name from the MCP config.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>COPILOT_MCP_ secret + mcp.json reference</b>: the only channel the agent MCP runtime reads.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A Codespaces secret</b> serves Codespaces, not the coding agent.</li><li><b>C plaintext apiKey in the repo</b> leaks the key to all readers.</li><li><b>D Actions secret + ${{ secrets }}</b> never reaches the agent MCP runtime.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Secrets have homes, and each home serves exactly one runtime. <b>Actions secrets</b> inject into workflow runs via secrets context. <b>Codespaces secrets</b> inject into your cloud dev environment. The <b>Copilot environment</b> (with the COPILOT_MCP_ prefix convention) injects into the coding agent and its MCP servers, referenced by variable name from mcp.json. Anything committed to git, a plaintext apiKey field, an instructions file, an agent config, is readable by everyone with repo access: that is a leak, not storage. <b>Example mcp.json:</b> the server entry sets apiKey to the COPILOT_MCP_MCP1 variable instead of the literal key. The literal key works today and leaks forever; the variable resolves only inside the agent runtime. When a question asks where an agent credential lives, first ask which runtime consumes it, then pick that runtime home.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":11,"topic":3,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\n<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image13\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou are evaluating how agent1 will behave after you implement the planned changes.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image14\" src=\"images/image11.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>Once MCP1 is configured, agent1 can invoke MCP1 tools autonomously.</td><td class=\"ynv once mcp1 is configured, agent1 can invoke mcp1 tools autonomously. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>agent1 can use the MCP resources and MCP prompts exposed by MCP1.</td><td class=\"ynv agent1 can use the mcp resources and mcp prompts exposed by mcp1. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>If new tools are added to MCP1, they will be available automatically to agent1.</td><td class=\"ynv if new tools are added to mcp1, they will be available automatically to agent1. yes\">Yes</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> An <b>MCP server</b> plugs external capabilities into the agent. MCP defines three surfaces: <b>tools</b> (callable functions), <b>resources</b> (readable data) and <b>prompts</b> (templates). The coding agent consumes configured tools; a remote server re-advertises its tool list every session.</div><div class=\"why\">Configured tools are autonomously callable and refresh dynamically; but MCP1 was configured <b>as a tool</b>, so resources/prompts are not on the table here.</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> MCP1 exposes a <b>create_ticket</b> tool, so agent1 can file tickets by itself. If Litware adds a <b>close_ticket</b> tool next week, it simply shows up. But the agent cannot open a ticket attachment (a resource) or fill in a ticket template (a prompt).</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> An MCP server exposes up to three surfaces and they are independent. <b>Tools</b> are callable functions: do this for me. <b>Resources</b> are readable data: let me see that. <b>Prompts</b> are reusable templates: fill this in. A client enables each surface separately, and the GitHub coding agent consumes configured tools. Remote servers re-advertise their catalog every session, so tools added server-side appear client-side with no config edit. <b>Example:</b> MCP1 offers create_ticket and deploy_build tools: agent1 calls them autonomously once configured. If Litware adds close_ticket next week, it just appears. But ticket attachments (resources) and ticket templates (prompts) were never configured as usable surfaces, so statements claiming the agent can use them are false even while tool statements are true. Always check which surface the question configured.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":12,"topic":3,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image16\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou need to make changes to repo1 to support the planned changes for the agents.<br />\nWhat should you modify?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.github/agents/*.agent.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.vscode/settings.json<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t.vscode/mcp.json<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t&lt;project&gt;/.mcp/server.json<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> VS Code Copilot Chat discovers MCP servers from <b>mcp.json</b>. A workspace-level <b>.vscode/mcp.json</b> can be committed to git, so one file configures every developer and every agent centrally. Per-user settings and per-agent profiles cannot do that.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>.vscode/mcp.json</b>: committable, central, repo-wide.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A .agent.md files</b> are per-agent personas, not MCP transport config.</li><li><b>B settings.json</b> is per-user local state: the opposite of central.</li><li><b>D &lt;project&gt;/.mcp/server.json</b> is project-scoped coding-agent config, not repo-wide Chat config.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> VS Code discovers MCP servers from <b>mcp.json</b> files in a fixed precedence: user profile settings (only you), then workspace <b>.vscode/mcp.json</b> (everyone opening this repo). Only the workspace file can be committed to git, which makes it the central lever: one file, whole team, all agents and developers, versioned and reviewable like code. Agent profile files (.agent.md) describe personas, and .vscode/settings.json holds local editor preferences: neither transports MCP servers. The &lt;project&gt;/.mcp/server.json style path scopes a single project coding-agent setup, not repo-wide Chat. <b>Example:</b> committing .vscode/mcp.json with the Learn server entry means a new hire opens repo1 and immediately validates designs against Microsoft Learn, with zero personal setup. That is what centrally means.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":13,"topic":3,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository.<br />\nYou use the GitHub Copilot CLI to run an agentic workflow from the terminal.<br />\nDuring execution, the conversation history approaches the context limit. The agent cannot continue the current session unless the amount of retained context is reduced.<br />\nYou need to continue the current session without losing all the prior progress.<br />\nWhich Copilot CLI slash command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/context<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/yolo<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/clear<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/compact<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> LLM CLIs work inside a fixed <b>context window</b>. Long agentic sessions fill it. <b>/compact</b> summarizes the conversation so far into a short brief and keeps going; /context only shows scope, /yolo only controls approvals.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>/compact</b>: same work, smaller context, nothing lost.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A /context</b> views scope; it frees nothing.</li><li><b>B /yolo</b> auto-approves tools; unrelated to context pressure.</li><li><b>C /clear</b> discards history instead of preserving it as a summary.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Language models read only a fixed-size <b>context window</b>: everything older gets pushed out, and the agent starts forgetting your goals, then hallucinating. Long agentic sessions (reproducing failures, iterating on approaches) fill the window fast. The CLI slash commands each do one chore: <b>/compact</b> summarizes the conversation so far into a compact brief and continues from it, <b>/context</b> shows what is currently loaded, <b>/clear</b> throws history away, <b>/yolo</b> toggles auto-approval. <b>Example:</b> after two hours of debugging, the CLI warns the context is nearly full. /compact distills 200 tool calls into 'tried X, failed at Y, next: Z' and work continues with full memory of conclusions. /clear would also free space, but it amputates the conclusions too. Compact preserves, clear discards: that is the whole difference.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":14,"topic":3,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository.<br />\nAn agent opens pull requests to the main branch.<br />\nYou need to ensure that changes to .github/workflows/* and /infra/* require approval from designated reviewers before merge.<br />\nWhat should you configure?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta ruleset and an agents.md file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta ruleset and a .copilotignore file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta branch protection rule and a CODEOWNERS file<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta branch protection rule and copilot-instructions.md<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Two GitHub mechanisms combine here. A <b>branch protection rule</b> can require pull-request reviews before merge on chosen paths; a <b>CODEOWNERS</b> file names who must review which paths. Together: designated reviewers must approve workflow/infra changes.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Protection rule + CODEOWNERS</b>: gate plus designated approvers.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A agents.md</b> is not an enforcement file.</li><li><b>B .copilotignore</b> only hides files from Copilot; it enforces no approval.</li><li><b>D copilot-instructions.md</b> guides agent behavior but cannot force human approval.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Protecting sensitive paths needs two cooperating mechanisms. A <b>branch protection rule (or ruleset)</b> is the lock: it can require pull-request reviews, status checks and named reviewers before merge on matching paths such as .github/workflows/* and /infra/*. A <b>CODEOWNERS</b> file is the keyholder list: lines mapping paths to teams or users who must review. Either alone is half a solution: a review requirement with no designated reviewer can be satisfied by anyone; designated owners with no enforcement can be bypassed. <b>Example CODEOWNERS:</b> a line assigning /infra/* to the security team plus a rule requiring CODEOWNERS review means every infra change waits for security approval, and admins see exactly who is responsible. agents.md is not a GitHub file, .copilotignore only hides files from Copilot suggestions, and copilot-instructions.md guides the agent but cannot force a human to approve.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/customizing-your-repository/about-code-owners\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CODEOWNERS</a></li></ul>"},{"n":15,"topic":3,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the following GrtHub Copilot CLI command in a Bash script.<br />\n<img title=\"image17\" src=\"images/image13.png\"><br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image18\" src=\"images/image14.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>The command will prevent runaway loops.</td><td class=\"ynv the command will prevent runaway loops. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>The command will allow the agent to use all tools.</td><td class=\"ynv the command will allow the agent to use all tools. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>The command will allow targeted human intervention at key decision points.</td><td class=\"ynv the command will allow targeted human intervention at key decision points. no\">No</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> This unattended script runs <b>copilot --autopilot --yolo --max-autopilot-continues 10</b>. <b>autopilot</b> = keep working without asking, <b>--yolo</b> = pre-approve every tool, <b>max-autopilot-continues</b> = hard cap on iterations.</div><div class=\"why\">The cap kills runaway loops; yolo unlocks every tool; but fully-automatic plus fully-approved leaves zero points where a human could step in.</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> without the cap, a dependency updater that always finds one more outdated package could loop forever; <b>--max-autopilot-continues 10</b> forces it to stop and report after 10 rounds.</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Unattended automation combines three CLI switches with three jobs. <b>--autopilot</b> tells the agent to keep working without asking. <b>--yolo</b> pre-approves every tool so nothing pauses for confirmation. <b>--max-autopilot-continues N</b> caps how many autonomous rounds may run: the emergency brake. The script also uses set -euo pipefail so the shell itself fails fast on errors. <b>Example:</b> 'update dependencies, run tests, open a PR' with --yolo lets the agent run package managers and git without prompts. If a dependency conflict makes it retry forever, the continue-cap stops it at 10 rounds instead of burning budget all night. Prevention of runaway loops is Yes, all-tools access is Yes, and precisely because nothing asks, targeted human intervention is No: autonomy and oversight are traded against each other, and this command spends all of it on autonomy.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":16,"topic":3,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions for CI.<br />\nYour team is piloting the GitHub Copilot coding agent to autonomously create branches and open pull requests. The repository follows trunk-based development that uses main as the default branch.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent meets the following requirements:<br />\nChanges to main can occur only by using pull requests that have at least one approval.<br />\nWhen a pull request is opened, a validation workflow runs, and the agent can still create branches and open pull requests autonomously.<br />\nHow should you configure the repository? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image20\" src=\"images/image16.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Branch protection (main): <b>Enabled</b><br>Validation trigger: <b>on: pull_request</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Trunk-based development = all work lands on main via short-lived PRs. The agent must be free to branch and open PRs, but main itself only moves through reviewed, CI-validated merges.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Enabled</b> bans direct pushes and demands PR + approval; <b>on: pull_request</b> validates every agent PR before merge.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>Disabled</b> or <b>bypass for direct pushes</b> re-opens unreviewed pushes.</li><li><b>on: push</b> fires on direct pushes and misses PR gating.</li><li><b>on: schedule / workflow_dispatch</b> are not PR-driven, so validation would not gate merges.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Trunk-based development means one shared <b>main</b> branch plus short-lived feature branches; main moves only through pull requests. Two settings implement that. The <b>branch protection rule</b> on main set to Enabled (require PR before merging, require approvals) makes direct pushes impossible: every change, human or agent, arrives as a PR. The workflow <b>trigger on: pull_request</b> makes validation automatic: each opened or updated PR runs tests and lint, and merging stays blocked until green. The agent keeps full autonomy to create branches and open PRs: protection constrains main, never the agent workspace. <b>Example:</b> the agent pushes three commits to copilot/fix-login. Each push triggers validation; reviewers see green checks plus one required approval; only then does Squash and merge unlock. on: push would run on direct pushes (which protection forbids anyway), schedule/dispatch would validate at random times instead of gating each PR.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li></ul>"},{"n":17,"topic":3,"q":9,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a repository on github.com that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nYou also use the GitHub Copilot CLI locally to reproduce failures and continue the same work from your terminal.<br />\nYou need to verify the current token usage.<br />\nWhich Copilot CLI slash command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/compact<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/diff<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/usage<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\t/context<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The CLI exposes slash commands for session chores: <b>/usage</b> prints token consumption, /compact shrinks context, /diff shows pending changes, /context shows what the model can see.</div><div class=\"why\">Token usage readout = <b>/usage</b>.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A /compact</b> manages context, <b>B /diff</b> shows changes, <b>D /context</b> shows scope: none reports tokens.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Copilot usage is metered in <b>tokens</b> (roughly word pieces the model reads and writes), and premium models and long agentic sessions consume budget fast. When local behavior must match the cloud agent run, you reproduce in the CLI and need to know where the budget stands. The CLI exposes one command per chore: <b>/usage</b> prints current token consumption, <b>/compact</b> shrinks context, <b>/diff</b> shows uncommitted changes, <b>/context</b> shows loaded context. <b>Example:</b> mid-reproduction you run /usage and see premium requests nearly exhausted: you switch the remaining exploration to a cheaper model instead of hitting a wall mid-task. Reaching for /compact would summarize the session (useful, different chore), /diff would show files (different question), /context would show scope (different question). Name the chore, pick the slash.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":18,"topic":3,"q":10,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYour team uses a remote GitHub Model Context Protocol (MCP) server for workflows in the software development life cycle (SDLC).<br />\nYou need to commit a workspace-scoped MCP configuration to ensure that GitHub Copilot can connect to the GitHub-hosted MCP endpoint and authenticate by using a GitHub personal access token (PAT).<br />\nHow should you complete the mcp.json configuration file? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image22\" src=\"images/image18.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"type: <b>http</b><br>Authorization: <b>Bearer ${env:GITHUB_PAT}</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> An MCP client config names each server with a transport <b>type</b> (http for a hosted HTTPS endpoint, stdio for a local process) plus auth headers. GitHub personal access tokens authenticate with the <b>Bearer</b> scheme.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>http</b> matches the hosted endpoint; <b>Bearer</b> matches PAT auth.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>stdio / local</b> describe process-local servers, not api.githubcopilot.com.</li><li><b>Token / x-api-key / Digest</b> are not the GitHub PAT scheme.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> An MCP client configuration entry has two load-bearing fields. <b>type</b> names the transport: <b>http</b> for a hosted HTTPS endpoint (a URL you call), <b>stdio</b> for a local process the client spawns, <b>local</b> for on-machine servers. The GitHub MCP lives at a URL, so it is http. <b>Authorization</b> carries credentials, and GitHub personal access tokens use the <b>Bearer</b> scheme: the literal word Bearer, a space, then the token (here read from the GITHUB_PAT environment variable so the secret never lands in the file). <b>Example entry:</b> github server, type http, url the copilot MCP endpoint, headers Authorization Bearer plus the PAT variable. Swap in stdio and the client tries to spawn a local process that does not exist. Swap in Token or x-api-key and GitHub rejects the scheme with authentication errors. Transport must match where the server lives; scheme must match what the server speaks.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":19,"topic":3,"q":11,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent named CodeAgent. The .agent.md file of CodeAgent contains the following YAML frontmatter.<br />\n---<br />\nname: CodeAgent<br />\ndescription: Performs repository analysis and code review tasks.<br />\ntools: ['edit', 'execute', 'read', 'search']<br />\n---<br />\nYou need to issue a GitHub Copilot CLI command that preserves execution velocity for read-only tasks by eliminating approval prompts for low-risk tools. The solution must ensure that high-risk tools that can make changes remain available but still require explicit user approval before running.<br />\nWhich command should you run?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --deny-tool 'edit,execute'<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-all-tools<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-tool 'read,search'<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcopilot agent run CodeAgent --allow-tool 'read,search' --deny-tool 'edit,execute'<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>--allow-tool</b> pre-approves named tools (no more prompts for them); <b>--deny-tool</b> removes tools entirely. Unlisted tools stay usable but keep prompting. Read and search are low-risk; edit and execute are high-risk.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>--allow-tool 'read,search'</b>: fast reads, while edit/execute remain available behind explicit approval.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A deny-only</b> still prompts for reads: no velocity gain.</li><li><b>B --allow-all-tools</b> also pre-approves edit/execute: unsafe.</li><li><b>C plain</b> prompts for everything: slow.</li><li><b>E + --deny</b> deletes edit/execute access, violating remain-available.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The <b>copilot agent run</b> command launches headless agents with two opposite flags. <b>--allow-tool</b> names tools that run without approval prompts: velocity for safe operations. <b>--deny-tool</b> removes tools entirely: they cannot run even with approval. Tools you neither allow nor deny keep working but keep prompting: available yet supervised. The requirement has two halves pulling in opposite directions: read-only tasks must fly (no prompts on read/search), high-risk tools must stay usable but supervised (edit/execute available, approval-gated). <b>Example:</b> with --allow-tool 'read,search', a triage sweep across 200 files never pauses, while the first edit still stops and asks. Add --deny-tool 'edit,execute' and that first edit becomes impossible instead of supervised: the agent reports it cannot complete the task. --allow-all-tools removes every gate including the safety ones. Match each requirement half to the flag that serves exactly it.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":20,"topic":3,"q":12,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions for CI on pull requests.<br />\nYou have a GitHub Copilot coding agent that opens pull requests for backlog items, and your company requires automated checks for agent-generated changes.<br />\nYou plan to standardize success criteria so that pull requests created by agents only succeed when unit tests pass and CodeQL analysis completes.<br />\nYou need to configure a GitHub Actions workflow that runs on pull requests, executes unit tests, and performs CodeQL analysis.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image24\" src=\"images/image20.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Run tests: <b>npm test</b><br>Prepare CodeQL: <b>init</b><br>Run CodeQL: <b>analyze</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> This repo tests with npm, and GitHub code scanning runs CodeQL as a fixed two-step action pair: <b>init</b> prepares the database, <b>analyze</b> runs the queries. upload-sarif exists only for third-party scanners.</div><div class=\"why\">npm runs this repo suite; init then analyze is the only valid CodeQL sequence.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>build</b> compiles instead of testing.</li><li><b>python -m unittest</b> is the wrong ecosystem.</li><li><b>upload-sarif</b> belongs to third-party SARIF uploads, not the native CodeQL flow.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> This workflow standardizes agent PR quality gates: unit tests plus security scanning. The test job runs the repo suite with <b>npm test</b> because this is a Node repository (a Python repo would use its own runner; build would only compile). Security scanning uses the GitHub <b>CodeQL action</b>, which always runs as an ordered pair: <b>init</b> builds the code database (understanding the code), then <b>analyze</b> executes the security queries against it (judging the code). Reversing or skipping one breaks the pair. <b>upload-sarif</b> belongs to a different story: importing results from third-party scanners into GitHub code scanning. With the native CodeQL action there is no SARIF file to upload; init plus analyze already delivers findings to the security tab. Read each box as asking which phase of which tool, then place the phase name.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/code-scanning/introduction-to-code-scanning/about-code-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Code scanning / CodeQL</a></li></ul>"},{"n":21,"topic":3,"q":13,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nEngineers assign issues to Copilot, and Copilot creates draft pull requests. The engineers start tasks either by assigning issues on github.com or by using GitHub Copilot Chat in an IDE. Reviewers request updates by leaving pull request comments.<br />\nYou discover that sometimes, Copilot uses outdated requirements after a reviewer posts an updated instruction in a pull request comment, and, in several cases, Copilot fails to resume work from the comment.<br />\nYou need to ensure that iteration requests are applied to the correct pull request session and are processed consistently.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the reviewer has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to mention @copilot in the pull request comments.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the agent has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to mention @github in the task comments.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnsure that the agent has write access to the repository. Instruct the reviewer to unassign and reassign the original issue to Copilot.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart a new task from Copilot Chat in the IDE. Instruct the reviewer to mention @github in the task comments.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> After the coding agent opens a PR, reviewers iterate by commenting. For the comment to reach the <b>same agent session</b>, the reviewer must be able to act on the repo (<b>write access</b>) and must ping the agent handle (<b>@copilot</b>) inside the <b>PR comments</b>. Anything else starts or targets the wrong session.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Write access + @copilot in PR comments</b>: routable, same-session iteration.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B</b>: agent-side access is not the gap; @github in task comments targets the wrong place.</li><li><b>C</b> unassign/reassign spawns a new session and drops iteration context.</li><li><b>D</b> a fresh Chat task is disconnected from the PR session.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> After the coding agent opens a PR, reviewers rarely accept the first draft: they request changes. Those follow-ups must reach the <b>same agent session</b> (which holds all context) rather than starting a fresh one. GitHub routes by mention and place: commenting <b>@copilot inside the PR comments</b> addresses the session that owns the PR. Two preconditions make it work. The commenter needs <b>write access</b>: without it GitHub ignores the instruction. And the mention must be <b>@copilot</b> (the agent), in the <b>PR</b> (its workplace): @github elsewhere addresses different machinery. <b>Example:</b> 'please also handle empty carts, @copilot' under the PR triggers a new commit on the same branch. Unassigning and reassigning the issue would spawn a blank session that redoes discovery; a fresh Chat task is disconnected from the PR entirely. Route, do not restart.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":22,"topic":3,"q":14,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYour company uses GitHub Copilot custom agents in Microsoft Visual Studio Code.<br />\nThe company also uses the Copilot coding agent on GitHub issues.<br />\nYou have a file named .planner.agent.md that defines an agent named planner.planner has tools set to ['search', 'read', 'fetch']. There are explicit instructions NOT to write or modify any code. The file also defines a handoff labeled Start Implementation to an agent named implementer and sets send to false.<br />\nDevelopers report that after the planner agent produces a plan, implementation sometimes starts immediately in the same conversation, and code changes appear without an explicit agent switch.<br />\nWhen Copilot-created pull requests stall, maintainers review the pull request timeline and session logs. Several stalled sessions show outbound network commands blocked by a firewall, and the repositories do NOT contain a .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image26\" src=\"images/image22.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>The planner config explicitly prevents code changes in the same conversation.</td><td class=\"ynv the planner config explicitly prevents code changes in the same conversation. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>The planner violates its tools via internet access; removing search fixes it.</td><td class=\"ynv the planner violates its tools via internet access; removing search fixes it. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>Repo-level copilot-instructions requiring tests+linters mitigates failing-CI stalls.</td><td class=\"ynv repo-level copilot-instructions requiring tests+linters mitigates failing-ci stalls. yes\">Yes</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The planner allows only search/read/fetch plus written orders never to write code: that <b>is</b> explicit prevention (the observed edits are non-compliance, not config). Web fetching via the allowed <b>fetch</b> tool is legitimate, so network blocks are a firewall problem, while failing CI is a test-discipline problem.</div><div class=\"why\">Config forbids edits (Yes) · fetch is allowed so search-removal fixes nothing (No) · test/linter gates attack failing CI directly (Yes).</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> the planner with only search/read/fetch is like a chef with no stove: it can write the recipe (plan) but physically cannot cook (edit). The handoff button then moves you to the implementer agent, which does have a stove.</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> This scenario packs three independent mini-lessons. First, a planner profile with only <b>search/read/fetch</b> plus written orders never to write code is explicitly fenced: no edit tool exists and instructions forbid writing, so any code appearing in the same conversation is model non-compliance, not configuration intent. Second, web access via the allowed <b>fetch</b> tool is legitimate: firewall-blocked outbound shell commands are an environment problem, and deleting <b>search</b> only blinds codebase lookup without touching network behavior. Third, a <b>repo-level copilot-instructions.md</b> applies to every agent run in the repo, so a 'run tests and linters before pushing' rule fires on every PR and directly starves the failing-CI stall pattern. <b>Example fix chain:</b> keep the planner fenced, allowlist the firewall for the runner, add the instructions file: each fix targets exactly one statement, which is why the answers are Yes, No, Yes.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":23,"topic":3,"q":15,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository.<br />\nDevelopers use the GitHub Copilot CLI and repository-scoped hooks under .github/hooks/*.json.<br />\nYou need to allow the Copilot CLI to automatically run low-risk Bash commands. The solution must prevent the autonomous execution of high-risk commands, such as sudo, rm -rf /, and curl ... | bash.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a userPromptSubmitted hook that logs prompts and exits nonzero.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd .github/hooks/logs/ to .gitignore.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a sessionStart hook that prints a policy banner.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a preToolUse hook that returns permissionDecision: \"deny\".<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> CLI <b>hooks</b> under .github/hooks/*.json intercept tool use. <b>preToolUse</b> runs before the command and can veto it with permissionDecision deny; userPromptSubmitted only observes prompts and sessionStart only runs once at launch.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>preToolUse + deny</b>: auto-allow the safe, block sudo / rm -rf / / curl-pipe-bash.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A userPromptSubmitted</b> with nonzero exit breaks the session instead of gating one command.</li><li><b>B gitignore</b> hides logs; it enforces nothing.</li><li><b>C sessionStart banner</b> is cosmetic text with no blocking power.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot CLI reads repository-scoped hooks from <b>.github/hooks/*.json</b>. Each hook type watches a different moment. <b>preToolUse</b> intercepts a tool or Bash command before it runs and can allow, prompt, or deny it via <b>permissionDecision</b>: the enforcement point. <b>userPromptSubmitted</b> sees your prompt text (good for logging, useless for gating commands, and a nonzero exit kills the whole session). <b>sessionStart</b> runs once at launch: banners, not enforcement. <b>Example policy:</b> a preToolUse hook receives every Bash command, auto-allows npm test and git status, and returns deny for anything matching sudo, rm -rf / or a curl pipe into bash. Low-risk work flows untouched, high-risk commands die before executing. Logging prompts or printing a banner never stops a command: only a pre-execution decision point can.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":24,"topic":3,"q":16,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYour company uses Microsoft Visual Studio Code and GitHub Copilot Chat.<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses main as the default branch. The repository contains a workspace custom agent stored at .github/agents/release-notes.agent.md.<br />\nA developer switches to a branch named branch1 where the agent file does NOT exist. In the same session, the developer switches to a user profile named profile1.profile1 contains a custom agent file named release-notes.agent.md that has user-invokable set to false.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image28\" src=\"images/image24.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>On main, the agent appears as release-notes.</td><td class=\"ynv on main, the agent appears as release-notes. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>On branch2, the workspace release-notes agent is available.</td><td class=\"ynv on branch2, the workspace release-notes agent is available. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>profile1 agent (user-invokable: false) appears and overrides the workspace agent.</td><td class=\"ynv profile1 agent (user-invokable: false) appears and overrides the workspace agent. no\">No</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> VS Code discovers workspace agents from the <b>checked-out branch</b> (.github/agents/*.agent.md) and profile agents from your user profile. <b>user-invokable: false</b> hides a profile agent from the dropdown entirely.</div><div class=\"why\">Present on main (Yes) · absent on branch2 (No) · hidden profile agent overrides nothing (No).</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> on main the Chat agent picker lists <b>release-notes</b>; check out branch2 (file absent) and it vanishes; a profile agent with user-invokable:false never appears in any picker.</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> VS Code discovers custom agents from two places with a visibility rule each. <b>Workspace agents</b> come from <b>.github/agents/*.agent.md on the currently checked-out branch</b>: switch branches and the set changes, because different branches contain different files. The dropdown shows each under its file-derived display name. <b>Profile agents</b> come from your user profile across all repos, but only if <b>user-invokable</b> is not false: false hides the agent everywhere, so it can neither appear nor override a same-named workspace agent. <b>Example:</b> on main the picker lists release-notes (file present). Check out branch2 where the file was never committed: the entry vanishes, because discovery reads the branch, not your memory. Flip to profile1 whose copy says user-invokable: false: nothing appears and nothing overrides. Branch controls workspace visibility; the flag controls profile visibility.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":25,"topic":4,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses a custom GitHub Copilot coding agent defined in the rollout-bot.agent.md file.<br />\nYou need to update a workflow so that agent-profile changes can be rolled back by reverting a single commit and rerunning the workflow. The workflow must check out the exact commit being deployed and apply the agent profile from the repository at that commit.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image30\" src=\"images/image26.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Checkout ref: <b>${{ inputs.ref }}</b><br>Profile path: <b>.github/agents/rollout-bot.md</b><br>Deploy arg: <b>inputs.ref</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Rollback-by-revert works only if the workflow deploys <b>exactly the ref you name</b> (workflow_dispatch input) and reads the agent profile from the repo <b>at that same ref</b>. Workspace agent profiles conventionally live under .github/agents/.</div><div class=\"why\">inputs.ref pins the commit end-to-end; the profile path follows the workspace convention.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>github.ref</b> resolves to the workflow own ref, not your chosen deploy ref.</li><li><b>agents/rollout-bot.md</b> is the wrong directory.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Rolling back an agent-profile change must be one revert plus one rerun, which demands the workflow deploy <b>exactly the ref you name</b> and read the profile <b>from the repo at that ref</b>. A <b>workflow_dispatch input</b> (here ref: commit SHA, tag or branch) carries your choice into the run as <b>inputs.ref</b>. Checkout with ref inputs.ref materializes that exact commit; validating the profile path and deploying from it keeps everything pinned to the same snapshot. Contrast <b>github.ref</b>: the ref that triggered the workflow run itself (usually main), which silently deploys the wrong commit. And workspace agent profiles conventionally live under <b>.github/agents/</b>, so that path prefix is load-bearing too. <b>Example:</b> dispatch with ref abc123, find a bad prompt, git revert the commit, rerun with the parent SHA: two clicks, fully deterministic, because no step ever resolved 'latest' on its own.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":26,"topic":4,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise organization that has Copilot memory enabled.<br />\nYou create a new repository.<br />\nWhat are two ways that memories will be deleted from the repository? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tmanually by repository members<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen the repository is archived<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen the code that created the memory is deleted<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tautomatically after 28 days<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\twhen pull requests are merged<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A, D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Copilot Memory</b> stores repo facts (conventions, build commands) and personal preferences. Two documented lifecycle rules: owners/admins can <b>manually delete</b> repo facts, and any entry <b>unused for 28 days is auto-deleted</b> (use resets the timer).</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Manual deletion + 28-day expiry</b>: the two documented deletion paths.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B archiving</b> deletes nothing by itself; inactive repos merely let entries expire via the 28-day rule.</li><li><b>C code deletion</b>: stale entries are ignored on validation, not auto-deleted.</li><li><b>E merging PRs</b> never deletes; closed-unmerged PRs can even create memories.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> <b>Copilot Memory</b> stores two things: <b>repository-level facts</b> (conventions, build commands, architecture: shared with everyone having memory access in that repo) and <b>user-level preferences</b> (your personal style: only yours, across repos). Two documented deletion paths exist. <b>Manual deletion:</b> repo owners/admins review and delete repo facts under Settings, Copilot, Memory; users delete their own preferences in personal settings. <b>Automatic expiry:</b> any entry unused for 28 days is deleted (each successful use resets the clock). <b>Example:</b> a fact 'deploy via ./scripts/ship.sh' nobody triggers for a month quietly disappears; an obsolete fact you spot today you delete by hand in settings. Archiving a repo deletes nothing by itself (inactivity just lets the 28-day rule bite), deleting source code merely makes the fact ignored at validation time, and merging PRs never deletes: in fact closed-unmerged PRs can create memories.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot Memory (concepts)</a></li></ul>"},{"n":27,"topic":4,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub Enterprise organization that uses GitHub Copilot.<br />\nYou discover that GitHub Copilot Chat responses in Microsoft Visual Studio Code are influenced by earlier, unrelated troubleshooting prompts from the same conversation.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the Copilot Chat conversation context is limited to information relevant to the current work item. The solution must minimize effort.<br />\nWhat should you do? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image32\" src=\"images/image28.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Previous prompt irrelevant: <b>Start a new chat.</b><br>Stay focused in IDE: <b>Open related files and close unrelated files.</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Copilot Chat builds context from <b>history + open files</b>. Old troubleshooting turns pollute answers; the cheapest reset is a fresh chat, and curating open files steers every future answer with zero configuration.</div><div class=\"why\">New chat = minimal-effort reset; working-set curation = continuous focus.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Deleting memories destroys useful repo knowledge.</li><li>Deleting one request is fiddly and never fully resets context.</li><li>Agent mode changes behavior instead of scoping context.</li><li>Disabling all features is overkill for a context problem.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Copilot Chat answers from two context sources: <b>conversation history</b> (everything said so far) and <b>open files / working set</b> (what the model may treat as relevant code). Old troubleshooting turns pollute history: the model keeps borrowing assumptions from a dead problem. Deleting memories destroys durable repo knowledge to fix a transient context problem. Deleting one request is fiddly and never fully resets. Switching to agent mode changes behavior, not context. <b>Example:</b> after an hour debugging flaky timeouts, you pivot to writing docs. The model keeps suggesting retry logic because the history screams timeouts. Start a new chat: one click, clean history, memories intact. Then close the timeout files and open only the docs: every future answer now draws on the docs instead. Reset history plus curate the working set: the complete focus recipe.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":28,"topic":4,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYour company uses GitHub Copilot Enterprise.<br />\nDevelopers use GitHub Copilot agent mode in Microsoft Visual Studio Code on their laptops and Copilot Chat on github.com when they are away from their laptops.<br />\nWhen switching between environments, the developers notice that agent workflows lose continuity because the tools available in Visual Studio Code are unavailable on github.com.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent tools and state are available consistently across environments and can be used from any device without local setup.<br />\nWhat should you do for each requirement? To answer, drag the appropriate actions to the correct requirements. Each action may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image34\" src=\"images/image30.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Cross-device tools, no local setup: <b>GitHub-hosted MCP server</b>.<br>Org-wide tools everywhere: <b>Enable MCP servers in Copilot policy</b>.<div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Local MCP servers (Docker, /mcp add) exist only on one laptop, so github.com sessions lose them. A <b>GitHub-hosted MCP server</b> travels with the account, and the <b>org Copilot policy</b> switch turns servers on for every environment at once.</div><div class=\"why\">Hosted = zero-setup everywhere; policy = one central switch.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Docker/local MCP needs per-machine setup and is absent on github.com.</li><li>/mcp add is per-device manual work.</li><li>Changing mode does not unify tools.</li><li>Disabling toolsets removes tools instead of unifying them.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> MCP servers come in two flavors with opposite portability. <b>Local servers</b> (Docker containers, CLIs added via /mcp add) live on one laptop: configured fast, but gone the moment you open github.com on a tablet. <b>GitHub-hosted MCP servers</b> live in the cloud attached to your account: identical toolset in VS Code and on github.com with zero local setup. Orthogonal to hosting, the <b>organization Copilot policy</b> is the central switch enabling MCP servers across every Copilot environment at once. <b>Example:</b> a team puts its ticket-system MCP on a hosted server and flips the policy on. A developer starts triage in VS Code, continues from a phone browser at lunch with the same ticket tools, and IT never configured a laptop. Docker would strand the tools on one machine; /mcp add would repeat manual setup per device; changing Chat mode changes nothing about tool availability.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":29,"topic":4,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent to resolve issues and create draft pull requests.<br />\nYou assign an issue to Copilot. Copilot creates a draft pull request. The pull request timeline shows Copilot started work, followed by status updates. The most recent status update is 55 minutes old.<br />\nYou discover that the agent is no longer making progress.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the work resumes without redoing the completed steps or changing the previously chosen approach.<br />\nWhat should you do? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image36\" src=\"images/image32.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Confirm progress: <b>View session from the pull request</b>.<br>Resume work: <b>post a PR comment mentioning @copilot</b>.<div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A 55-minute-silent agent may just be stuck, not dead. <b>View session</b> shows its live state, and an <b>@copilot PR comment</b> nudges the same session to continue: completed steps and approach survive.</div><div class=\"why\">Observe first, then continue in place: no redo, no approach change.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Repo insights / local git log show code, not agent progress.</li><li>Close/reopen restarts and risks redoing work.</li><li>Marking ready is premature.</li><li>A new PR from Chat starts a new session.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> A silent agent is not necessarily a dead agent, so confirm before acting. <b>View session</b> (from the PR) streams the live session: running, waiting on approvals, or genuinely stuck. If it needs a push, comment <b>@copilot on the PR</b>: the same session continues with its completed steps, chosen approach and branch intact. The alternatives all destroy continuity: closing/reopening the issue, opening a fresh PR from Chat, or marking the draft ready all start over or misrepresent state; repo insights and local git log show code, never agent state. <b>Example:</b> status shows 'started work' then 55 minutes of silence. View session reveals it waiting on a workflow approval: click Approve and run, then comment '@copilot continue after CI' if needed. Observe first through the session, steer second through the PR: resume without redo.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":30,"topic":4,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nYour company restricts GitHub Actions secrets.<br />\nDevelopers need the Copilot coding agent to call an internal dependency-scanning API during its run. The API requires an access token.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the Copilot coding agent can use the token during execution without accessing the repository's Actions secrets and variables. The solution must prevent exposing the token in plaintext.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the token as an Actions repository secret.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd the token as a secret in the Copilot environment.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the token in a repository custom instructions file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore the token in the agent configuration file.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The coding agent runs outside Actions, so Actions secrets are invisible to it, and anything in git is public to readers. Short-lived credentials for the agent belong in the <b>Copilot environment secrets</b>.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Copilot environment secret</b>: the only secret store the agent runtime reads.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A Actions secret</b> serves workflows, not the agent.</li><li><b>C instructions file / D agent config</b> commit the token in plaintext: a leak.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The coding agent executes outside GitHub Actions, in its own runtime with its own credential channels. <b>Actions secrets</b> inject only into workflow runs. Files in git (custom instructions, agent configs, committed JSON) are readable by every repo reader. The <b>Copilot environment</b> is the agent runtime secret store: values placed there are visible to the agent and its tools and nowhere else. <b>Example:</b> the agent must call an internal API during its session. As an Actions secret it would sit unused while the agent fails with 401s; committed to copilot-instructions.md it would leak to every developer and every fork. As a Copilot environment secret the call succeeds and the token appears in no log, no diff, no clone. Rule of thumb: name the consuming runtime first, then pick its native secret home.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":31,"topic":5,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a private GitHub repository that has Copilot memory enabled.<br />\nSeveral developers who have write access to the repository make changes across multiple branches, including creating some pull requests that are later closed without merging.<br />\nYour team needs to understand how GitHub Copilot ensures that only task-relevant, up-to-date information influences code suggestions, even when older memories exist.<br />\nHow does Copilot manage memories?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot validates each memory's citations against the current branch before using the memory, and ignores the memory if the referenced code no longer exists.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot stores memories per user, ensuring that only the developer who created a memory can trigger the memory in future sessions.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot stores memories indefinitely until a repository administrator deletes them manually.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCopilot automatically blocks memory creation from pull requests that are closed without merging, to prevent outdated information from being stored.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Memories point at code (<b>citations</b>). Before each use, Copilot re-checks those citations against the current branch: moved or deleted code means the memory is skipped, so stale knowledge can never drive an answer.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Validate-then-ignore-stale</b>: freshness is enforced at use time.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B</b>: repository facts are shared with everyone having memory access, not locked to the creator.</li><li><b>C</b> contradicts the documented 28-day expiry of unused entries.</li><li><b>D</b> is backwards: closed-unmerged PRs can seed memories.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Memories would rot fast if Copilot trusted them blindly, so every memory carries <b>citations</b>: pointers to the code or words that support it. Before each use, Copilot re-checks repository-fact citations against the <b>current branch</b> and silently <b>ignores</b> entries whose code is gone: stale knowledge can never drive an answer, and ignoring is not deleting (the entry may validate again later or expire via the 28-day rule). <b>Example:</b> a fact 'retries live in net/retry.go' survives refactors that keep the file, and is skipped the week retry.go is deleted, without any admin action. Contrast the distractors: facts are shared repo-wide, not locked to their creator; nothing lives indefinitely; and closed-unmerged PRs can seed memories precisely because validation (not merge status) guards quality.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-memory\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot Memory (concepts)</a></li></ul>"},{"n":32,"topic":5,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image38\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to troubleshoot the issue reported by Ben.<br />\nWhat should you review?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GITHUB_TOKEN permissions block in the agent1 workflow<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe agent session log in the Agents panel<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GitHub Actions runner log for the session job<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe GitHub Actions usage metrics of repo1<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> An empty-but-successful PR means the agent did work that produced no diff: only its own narration explains why. The <b>Agents panel session log</b> records reasoning, tool calls and decisions; runner logs show infra output and metrics show aggregates.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Session log</b>: the sole artifact containing agent intent.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A GITHUB_TOKEN perms</b> describe auth scope, not behavior.</li><li><b>C runner log</b> shows infrastructure, not decisions.</li><li><b>D usage metrics</b> aggregate many sessions.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Diagnose empty-but-successful PRs by asking which artifact records <b>agent intent</b>, not infrastructure. The <b>Agents panel session log</b> is the agent own narration: what it read, decided, ran and why. The <b>Actions runner log</b> records the sandbox: checkouts, installs, exit codes. <b>GITHUB_TOKEN permissions</b> describe what the agent was allowed to touch. <b>Usage metrics</b> aggregate thousands of sessions. <b>Example:</b> the log shows 'explored auth/, found the SSO flag already handled in middleware, no diff needed, opened PR to report': mystery solved, the agent was right. The runner log would only show green steps; metrics would show one more successful session; permissions would show scopes. Match the question (why did it do nothing?) to the only artifact containing decisions.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":33,"topic":5,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image39\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nYou are evaluating the logs of the multi-agent workflow in repo2.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image40\" src=\"images/image34.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>The Github MCP server is enabled for the agent sessions.</td><td class=\"ynv the github mcp server is enabled for the agent sessions. yes\">Yes</td></tr><tr><td>Workspace isolation is being used by the agent sessions.</td><td class=\"ynv workspace isolation is being used by the agent sessions. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>The logs show exactly two agent sessions started.</td><td class=\"ynv the logs show exactly two agent sessions started. yes\">Yes</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Read the log literally: <b>Passing 1 MCP server(s) [github]</b> means the server reached the session (CLIMCPServerEnabled=false only gates extra CLI-side servers). <b>isolationEnabled: false</b> with no worktree means isolation never engaged. Sessions are the two distinct <b>isNewSession: true</b> IDs.</div><div class=\"why\">Server passed (Yes) · isolation off (No) · two session IDs (Yes).</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example lines:</b> <b>Passing 1 MCP server(s) to SDK: [github]</b> proves statement 1; <b>isolationEnabled: false</b> with no worktree disproves statement 2; two <b>Invoking... isNewSession: true</b> IDs (de04f0fe, 6852c5dc) prove statement 3.</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> CLI session logs reward literal reading: trust exact field values over vibes. <b>Passing 1 MCP server(s) to SDK: [github]</b> is positive proof the GitHub server reached the session; the nearby <b>CLIMCPServerEnabled=false</b> only gates additional CLI-side servers, a different list. <b>isolationEnabled: false</b> plus no worktree path is negative proof: isolation mode may have been selected, but it never engaged. Sessions are counted by distinct <b>isNewSession: true</b> identities, not by request lines (ccreq IDs are per-call, not per-session). <b>Example walk:</b> line 'Passing 1 MCP server(s) [github]' answers statement 1 Yes. 'isolationEnabled: false, worktreePath: undefined' answers statement 2 No. Two Invoking lines with de04f0fe and 6852c5dc answer statement 3 Yes. Commenters split because they skimmed; the fields decide.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":34,"topic":5,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have the following agent logs.<br />\n<img title=\"image42\" src=\"images/image36.png\"><br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image43\" src=\"images/image37.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>[Statement_2] The agent responded with messages.</td><td class=\"ynv [statement_2] the agent responded with messages. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>[Statement_3] The agent can edit files in the repository.</td><td class=\"ynv [statement_3] the agent can edit files in the repository. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>[Statement_1] The agent analyzed files in the repository.</td><td class=\"ynv [statement_1] the agent analyzed files in the repository. no\">No</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> This log contains only model calls and progress notes. <b>message 0 returned</b> literally means zero messages produced; <b>[panel/editAgent]</b> is a UI panel tag, not a file edit; and no line shows any file read, search or analysis.</div><div class=\"why\">No messages, no edits, no file analysis anywhere: all three No.</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> <b>message 0 returned</b> means the model call ended having produced zero messages: silence, not a reply. <b>[panel/editAgent]</b> names the VS Code side panel, not a file operation: zero evidence of repository edits.</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> This log is a masterclass in what absence proves. <b>message 0 returned. finish reason: [stop]</b> means a model call completed having produced zero messages: silence, not a reply, so 'responded with messages' is No no matter how many such lines exist. <b>[panel/editAgent]</b> is a VS Code UI panel tag identifying where the call originated, exactly like a window title: it evidences zero file operations, so 'can edit files' is No. The remaining lines are model identifiers (gpt-4o-mini, gpt-5.3-codex), progress notes and wrappers: compute happened, but no line shows a file read, search, or analysis operation, and <b>copilotmd</b> labels the request format, not a repository file. So 'analyzed files' is No. <b>Example mistake to avoid:</b> one commenter read panel/editAgent as editing and message-0 as messaging: in logs, names describe plumbing, and only verbs (read, edit, search, analyze with paths) describe work.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":35,"topic":5,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Copilot code review on pull requests.<br />\nYou plan to add repository-wide code review guidance that will apply to all files.<br />\nYou need Copilot code review to consistently apply the guidance during pull request reviews.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tModify the .github/pull_request_template.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate the instructions in the .github/agents/*.agent.md files.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a .github/instructions/copilot.instructions.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a .github/copilot-instructions.md file.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Copilot code review reads repo-wide guidance from <b>.github/copilot-instructions.md</b> (always applied), while <b>.github/instructions/*.instructions.md</b> files are path-scoped via applyTo, agent profiles scope single agents, and the PR template is human boilerplate.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>copilot-instructions.md</b>: the documented repo-wide home for review guidance.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A PR template</b> instructs human authors, not the reviewer.</li><li><b>B .agent.md profiles</b> scope one agent, not all reviews.</li><li><b>C instructions/*.instructions.md</b> applies only to matching paths.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Copilot code review accepts guidance from several files with different scopes, and scope decides. <b>.github/copilot-instructions.md</b> applies repo-wide to every review: the home for team-wide review policy. <b>.github/instructions/*.instructions.md</b> files apply only to paths matching their applyTo patterns: perfect for 'frontend rules for /frontend'. <b>.agent.md profiles</b> shape single agents, not the reviewer. <b>pull_request_template.md</b> coaches human authors filling in PR bodies. <b>Example:</b> the team wants 'every PR checked for missing tests and raw SQL'. That rule belongs in copilot-instructions.md so backend, docs and infra PRs all get it. Putting it in a path-scoped instructions file would silently skip half the repo; putting it in an agent profile would miss reviews entirely, since the reviewer is not that agent.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":36,"topic":5,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent and opens draft pull requests for assigned issues.<br />\nEvaluation results show that the agent repeatedly opens pull requests that modify .github/workflows/*.yml to bypass failing checks instead of fixing the underlying code.<br />\nYou need to ensure that the agent fixes the underlying code instead of bypassing the failing checks.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStart the GitHub Copilot CLI by using --allow-all.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpdate .github/copilot-instructions.md.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a branch ruleset.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tDisable GitHub Actions workflows for pull requests created by the agent.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The agent found a loophole: editing workflows silences failing checks. Blocking the loophole stops the symptom; <b>rewriting the repo instructions</b> (never touch workflows, fix the underlying code) cures the strategy.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Update copilot-instructions.md</b>: teach the right goal, not just block the wrong one.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A --allow-all</b> grants even more autonomy: worse.</li><li><b>C ruleset</b> blocks the bypass but the agent stalls instead of fixing code.</li><li><b>D disabling Actions</b> deletes validation entirely.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> When an agent games your checks, distinguish blocking the move from teaching the game. The agent edits workflows because its instructions never said the goal is fixed code with green checks; bypassing checks is, to it, a creative shortcut. A <b>branch ruleset</b> would block workflow edits, but the agent then stalls with failing tests and no permitted path forward. Disabling Actions removes the signal entirely. --allow-all adds autonomy to misbehavior. <b>Updating .github/copilot-instructions.md</b> with 'never modify CI to green it; fix the underlying code; failing checks mean the code is wrong' rewrites the objective function itself. <b>Example instruction block:</b> list forbidden strategies (editing workflows, deleting tests, marking failures tolerated) plus the required one (fix source, rerun, report). Agents optimize what you write down: write the real goal.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/custom-instructions\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom instructions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":37,"topic":5,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You use a GitHub Actions workflow to orchestrate a multi-agent draft-review process for pull requests. The workflow uploads a single combined review-package artifact at the end of each run.<br />\nYou discover that the review-package artifact is occasionally missing from the workflow run history, and you suspect that a user deleted it.<br />\nYou need to identify which user manually deleted a workflow run artifact and when the deletion occurred.<br />\nWhat should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe checks.delete_logs audit log event<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe pull request description and comment history<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe merge commit history<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe artifact.destroy audit log event<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> GitHub records security-relevant actions as named <b>audit log events</b> with actor and timestamp. Artifact deletion is the <b>artifact.destroy</b> event: filter for it to answer who deleted what and when.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>artifact.destroy</b>: the exact event for this question.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A checks.delete_logs</b> belongs to a different event family.</li><li><b>B PR text</b> and <b>C merge history</b> record discussion and commits, never artifact deletion.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> GitHub records security-relevant actions as structured <b>audit log events</b>, each stamped with actor, timestamp, repo and action name. Artifact deletion is the <b>artifact.destroy</b> event: filter the audit log for it and you get exactly who deleted which artifact when. The neighboring options live in different universes: <b>checks.delete_logs</b> concerns check-run logs, PR descriptions and comments narrate human discussion, merge history records commits. None stores deletion events. <b>Example investigation:</b> nightly-release.zip vanished. Audit log filtered to action:artifact.destroy shows deploy-bot deleted it Tuesday 03:12 during retention cleanup. Without the event filter you would grep PR prose forever and find nothing, because prose never records deletions. Name the event family first (artifact.*), then the verb (destroy).</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},{"n":38,"topic":5,"q":8,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses the GitHub Copilot coding agent.<br />\nCopilot creates a draft pull request for an assigned issue, and the pull request timeline shows Copilot started work.<br />\nAfter 70 minutes, the agent session log stops updating, and the pull request body status stops changing.<br />\nYou need to restart the agent so that it continues the task from the issue context and produces new commits to the existing draft pull request.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tWait for the agent to complete.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tMerge the draft pull request and mention @copilot on the merged pull request.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUnassign the issue from Copilot, and then reassign the issue to Copilot.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSelect Approve and run workflows in the pull request merge box.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A session that stopped (here: the 70-minute mark) will never finish on its own. The supported restart is <b>unassign + reassign the issue</b>: Copilot picks it up fresh from the same issue context. Commenting @copilot is for steering a live PR, not reviving a dead session.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Unassign/reassign</b>: the documented session restart.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A waiting</b> never resolves a stall.</li><li><b>B merging</b> an empty PR is wrong, and merged sessions are closed.</li><li><b>D Approve-and-run</b> gates workflows; it does not revive agents.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Agent sessions have two states needing different verbs. A <b>live</b> session (open PR, recent activity) is steered: comment @copilot on the PR. A <b>dead</b> session (long silence, e.g. past the 70-minute mark, no progress in View session) cannot be steered: it must be <b>restarted</b>, and the supported restart is unassign the issue from Copilot and reassign it, which spawns a fresh session from the same issue context. The wrong verbs misfire characteristically: waiting on a dead session waits forever; merging its empty PR ships nothing and closes the session; Approve-and-run gates workflows instead of reviving agents. <b>Example:</b> Friday session silent since Thursday. Unassign, reassign Monday: new session, same issue text, full history visible. Commenting @copilot on the stale PR instead would address a corpse.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":39,"topic":5,"q":9,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses a custom coding agent to run GitHub Actions workflows that create branches, open pull requests, and merge changes after required checks pass.<br />\nYou need to log all agent-initiated actions and ensure that the logs are retained for two years.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure audit log retention.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable log forwarding.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tEnable Git events in the audit log.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure audit log streaming to Azure Event Hubs.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> GitHub keeps audit history briefly; there is no 2-year retention knob. Long-lived compliance history requires <b>streaming audit events out</b> to your own sink (Azure Event Hubs, S3, etc.) as they happen.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Stream to Event Hubs</b>: the only path to multi-year immutable history.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A retention setting</b> with a multi-year value does not exist.</li><li><b>B log forwarding</b> names no compliant sink.</li><li><b>C Git events toggle</b> selects event types, not retention.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> GitHub keeps audit history briefly (weeks, not years) and offers no multi-year retention dial: 'configure retention to 2 years' is not a real knob. Compliance regimes that demand years of immutable evidence use <b>audit log streaming</b>: every event is forwarded as it happens to your own sink such as <b>Azure Event Hubs</b> (or S3, Splunk), where your retention policy, not GitHub, applies. 'Enable log forwarding' and 'Git events' name no durable multi-year sink; toggling event types changes what is recorded, not how long it survives. <b>Example architecture:</b> org streams to Event Hubs, which archives to immutable blob storage with a 7-year lock. Two years later an auditor asks who approved a production deploy: you query your archive, not GitHub, because GitHub forgot months ago. Retention questions are always answered by where events are copied, never by a setting that does not exist.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},{"n":40,"topic":5,"q":10,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have multiple GitHub Copilot coding agents that run tasks concurrently.<br />\nYou live stream the session log output and see the following.<br />\n<img title=\"image45\" src=\"images/image39.png\"><br />\nWhat is a possible cause of the error?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta postToolUse hook<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta GitHub app installation policy<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta preToolUse hook<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\ta Copilot firewall policy<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A <b>preToolUse</b> hook inspects each tool call before it runs and can deny it; the denial surfaces right in the live stream. postToolUse only observes completed calls, app policies govern installs, and firewall blocks look like network failures.</div><div class=\"why\">Mid-stream failure during a tool call = hook denial.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A postToolUse</b> runs after success; it cannot break a live call.</li><li><b>B app policy</b> governs installs/permissions, not runtime calls.</li><li><b>D firewall</b> produces network errors, a different shape.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Diagnose runtime failures by locating them on the timeline. A <b>preToolUse hook</b> runs synchronously inside the tool call: when it denies, the live stream breaks exactly there, mid-task, with the hook name in the error. That signature (failure during invocation, before any result) rules out the neighbors: <b>postToolUse</b> only observes completed calls and cannot break them; <b>app installation policies</b> fail at install/permission time, long before streaming; <b>firewall policies</b> fail network calls with network-shaped errors (timeouts, resets), not hook decisions. <b>Example:</b> the stream shows 'calling deploy-staging...' then 'denied by preToolUse guardrail: production deploys need approval'. The fix is a policy exception or an approval step, not debugging the network or reinstalling anything. Read the break point, name the gate at that point.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":41,"topic":5,"q":11,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You are analyzing the following agent logs.<br />\n<img title=\"image46\" src=\"images/image40.png\"><br />\nYou need to classify the error in a report for your company's CTO.<br />\nHow should you classify the error?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tContext Issue<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tReasoning error<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tTool misuse<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tNetwork issue<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Classify by where it broke: the agent called the Microsoft Docs MCP search tool and the <b>invocation itself failed before any result</b>. That is <b>tool misuse</b>: not bad reasoning (logic error, call succeeds), not a context problem (prompt/window sizing), not a network outage (transport failure).</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Tool misuse</b>: failed invocation, no result.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A context issue</b> would be sizing/scope of the prompt.</li><li><b>B reasoning error</b> means flawed logic with a working call.</li><li><b>D network issue</b> means the transport failed.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Error taxonomy for agent runs has four buckets; classify by where it broke, not how it feels. <b>Tool misuse:</b> the invocation itself failed before producing a result (bad arguments, disallowed target, MCP schema violation). <b>Reasoning error:</b> the call succeeded but the model logic was wrong (called the right tool for the wrong reason). <b>Context issue:</b> prompt, window or scope problems (too much, too little, wrong files loaded). <b>Network issue:</b> transport failures (DNS, TLS, timeouts). <b>Example:</b> the agent calls the Microsoft Docs search tool and the call fails immediately with no result: tool misuse, fix the invocation or permissions. Had it returned irrelevant docs, that would be reasoning (wrong query). Had the call never left the machine, network. Had the prompt exceeded limits, context. The log shows failure during invocation with zero output: bucket one.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/copilot/customization/mcp-servers\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP servers in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":42,"topic":5,"q":12,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have multiple GitHub Copilot coding agents that run tasks concurrently.<br />\nYou are monitoring the agents from the terminal by using the GitHub CLI.<br />\nAn agent appears stalled.<br />\nYou need to live stream the session log output.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task view and specify the --log and --jq parameters.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task view and specify the --log and --follow parameters.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task list and specify the --web parameter.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tRun gh agent-task list and specify the --status and --jq parameters.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The <b>gh agent-task</b> extension manages agent tasks. <b>view</b> targets one task, <b>--log</b> prints its session log, <b>--follow</b> keeps streaming like tail -f. --jq only queries static JSON; list only enumerates; --web opens a browser.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>view --log --follow</b>: one task, live log.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A --jq</b> queries a static snapshot: no streaming.</li><li><b>C/D list</b> enumerate tasks; --web opens a browser and --status filters: neither streams a log.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The <b>gh agent-task</b> extension splits task management into verbs with Unix-style flags. <b>list</b> enumerates tasks (--status filters, --web opens the browser view). <b>view</b> targets one task: <b>--log</b> prints its session log, <b>--follow</b> keeps the stream open like tail -f, <b>--jq</b> queries a static JSON snapshot. 'Live stream one session log' decomposes to view (one task) + log (its log) + follow (keep streaming). <b>Example:</b> gh agent-task view 42 --log --follow tails task 42 until you Ctrl-C. Swap --follow for --jq and you get one frozen JSON dump: perfect for scripting, useless for watching. Swap view for list and you enumerate tasks without ever seeing a log. Read the requirement as verb plus modifiers, then assemble exactly those flags.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":43,"topic":5,"q":13,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent that has completed a pull request for a security fix in your repository.<br />\nBefore merging, you need to evaluate the quality of the agent's work by using both automated evaluation signals and human review.<br />\nYou review the session log and the pull request.<br />\nWhat are two automated evaluation signals generated by the coding agent's built-in scanning tools? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tlinting errors from the repository's CI pipeline configured in GitHub Actions<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcomments from Copilot code review that suggest improvements to code patterns<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe detection of hardcoded secrets, such as API keys and tokens<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tthe median time to merge metric reported on the Copilot usage metrics dashboard<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCodeQL findings that identify security vulnerabilities in the generated code<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C, E</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The coding agent ships <b>built-in scanners</b> that judge generated code: <b>secret scanning</b> flags hardcoded keys/tokens, <b>CodeQL code scanning</b> flags vulnerabilities. Your CI lint, AI review prose and usage dashboards are useful but none is a built-in scanner signal.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Secret detection + CodeQL</b>: the two scanner signals.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A repo CI lint</b> is your pipeline, not built-in.</li><li><b>B review comments</b> are reviewer prose, not scanner output.</li><li><b>D time-to-merge</b> measures process, not this code.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> 'Automated evaluation signals' means machine verdicts about the code, and 'built-in scanning tools' means scanners GitHub ships and runs itself. Two qualify. <b>Secret scanning</b> detects hardcoded API keys and tokens: a credential in generated code is an objective defect no human must eyeball. <b>CodeQL code scanning</b> proves security properties (injection, auth flaws) with query-based analysis. The distractors fail the definition differently: <b>repo CI lint</b> is your pipeline, not built-in; <b>Copilot review comments</b> are AI prose suggestions, not scanner verdicts; <b>median time-to-merge</b> measures team process on a dashboard, saying nothing about this diff. <b>Example:</b> the agent PR adds aStripe key literal and a SQL-concatenated query: secret scanning flags the key, CodeQL flags the injection, CI lint stays green, review prose suggests style, the dashboard shows last month velocity. Only the first two evaluate this code automatically.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/secret-scanning/about-secret-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Secret scanning</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/code-security/code-scanning/introduction-to-code-scanning/about-code-scanning\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Code scanning / CodeQL</a></li></ul>"},{"n":44,"topic":5,"q":14,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise repository that runs an autonomous agent by using a GitHub Actions workflow. The workflow has the following jobs:<br />\nagent-run that generates trace.json and plan.md<br />\nreview that waits for human approval before continuing<br />\ndeploy that uses the outputs from agent-run<br />\nYou need to make the files inspectable in the GitHub Actions UI and ensure that the files are available to the review and deploy jobs.<br />\nWhat should you do in the workflow?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUse dependency caching to store trace.json and plan.md.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tUpload trace.json and plan.md as workflow artifacts in agent-run, and download the files inside the jobs.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCommit trace.json and plan.md back to the repository from agent-run.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tStore trace.json and plan.md on a network share and have later jobs retrieve them from the share.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Runners are ephemeral and jobs isolated: files pass between jobs only through <b>upload-artifact / download-artifact</b>. Caches are best-effort (for dependencies), commits pollute history, and network shares add infra runners cannot rely on.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Upload in agent-run, download in review/deploy</b>: the native handoff.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A caching</b> is best-effort dependency storage, not a file handoff.</li><li><b>C committing</b> generated files pollutes the repo.</li><li><b>D network share</b> is external infra with no availability guarantee.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> GitHub runners are ephemeral and jobs are isolated: when agent-run finishes, its filesystem evaporates, so later jobs (review, deploy) cannot see trace.json and plan.md unless the files travel through the native handoff: <b>upload-artifact</b> in the producer, <b>download-artifact</b> in each consumer. The alternatives fail on fitness, not syntax. <b>Dependency caching</b> is best-effort storage for dependencies that may miss: unacceptable for required inputs. <b>Committing</b> generated files pollutes history with machine output. A <b>network share</b> adds infrastructure runners cannot mount or trust. <b>Example:</b> agent-run uploads plan.md (50 KB, instant); review downloads it, renders the diff summary; deploy downloads it again to extract rollout steps. Same file, three jobs, zero repo pollution: artifacts are the clipboard between jobs.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-workflows/storing-workflow-data-as-artifacts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow artifacts</a></li></ul>"},{"n":45,"topic":5,"q":15,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  A developer uses the GitHub Copilot CLI in plan mode.<br />\nCopilot produces a plan.<br />\nWhat does Copilot do next?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tsaves the plan to plan.md<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tcreates a branch named /copilot/plan in the repository<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\topens a pull request that has the plan as a comment<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tbegins the implementation<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Plan mode</b> exists so nothing happens except planning: Copilot investigates and writes the plan to <b>plan.md</b>, then stops. Branches, PRs and implementation are all actions beyond planning.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>saves the plan to plan.md</b>: planning-only, by definition.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B branch / C PR / D implement</b> each contradict plan-mode semantics.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> CLI working modes form a ladder of commitment. Normal mode acts as it thinks. <b>Plan mode</b> thinks first, writes the thinking to <b>plan.md</b>, and stops: the file is the deliverable, reviewable in your editor, diffable, committable. Only your explicit next instruction moves it to branch, PR or implementation. Every other option describes climbing the ladder unasked: creating /copilot/plan branches, opening PRs with the plan as a comment, or beginning implementation all act beyond planning. <b>Example:</b> you run plan mode on 'migrate auth to the new provider'. It writes plan.md with phases, risks and test strategy, then idles. You review over coffee, adjust two phases, and only then say implement. Had it branched or PR-ed on its own, your review would chase moving code instead of judging a document. Planning-only is a promise, and plan.md is its receipt.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":46,"topic":6,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image47\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to resolve the issue of the agents generating conflicting output. The solution must meet the implementation guidelines.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd shared/config.yaml to a CODEOWNERS file that requires SG_Review approval before any changes can be merged.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure each agent to work on a separate branch and add a required status check that detects file-level overlap before either pull request can be merged.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure tools: ['read', 'search'] in both agent profiles to prevent either agent from writing files.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tConfigure a concurrency group on both agent workflows so that only one workflow runs at a time.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>B</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Three constraints must hold at once: agents run <b>concurrently</b>, on <b>isolated branches</b>, with <b>file conflicts caught before merge</b>. Only separate branches plus a required overlap-detecting status check satisfies all three.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Separate branches + overlap check</b>: parallel, concurrent, safe.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A CODEOWNERS</b> adds review but no branch isolation: outputs still collide.</li><li><b>C read-only tools</b> makes the agents unable to do their jobs.</li><li><b>D concurrency group</b> serializes the workflows, violating concurrency.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Parallel agents sharing one file need three guarantees simultaneously, and each distractor drops exactly one. <b>Isolated branches</b> give each agent its own workspace so edits never interleave mid-flight. <b>Concurrency</b> (both keep running) preserves speed: serializing via a concurrency group would be safe but slow, violating the requirements. <b>Pre-merge overlap detection</b> (a required status check comparing the two PRs file lists) catches the shared/config.yaml collision before either merges. <b>Example:</b> agent1 and agent2 branch separately and both touch shared/config.yaml. The check fails on both PRs with 'overlap detected': a human reconciles the file once, both rebase, both merge green. CODEOWNERS review alone would approve sequentially without ever isolating the work; read-only tools would prevent the work itself; a concurrency group would make one agent wait idle. Check which guarantee each option sacrifices.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Branch protection rules</a></li></ul>"},{"n":47,"topic":6,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that contains the following custom agent files:<br />\nA file named planner.agent.md that includes YAML frontmatter with a handoffs entry that has label: Start Implementation, agent: implementer, and prompt: Now implement the plan outlined above<br />\nA file named implementer.agent.md that is in the same directory as planner.agent.md and includes YAML frontmatter that has name: IMPLEMENTER<br />\nYou add a third agent file named review.agent.md.review.agent.md includes YAML frontmatter that has name: code-review.<br />\nYou make the following changes to planner.agent.md:<br />\nUpdate the existing handoff to include send: true and model: GPT-5.2 (copilot).<br />\nAdd a second handoff that has label: Run Review, agent: code-review, and prompt: Review the code changes made in the previous step.<br />\nNo other agent files are modified.<br />\nFor each of the following statements, select Yes if the statement is true. Otherwise, select No.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image48\" src=\"images/image41.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"<table class=\"yn\"><tr><th>Statement</th><th>Answer</th></tr><tr><td>The Run Review handoff resolves to review.agent.md.</td><td class=\"ynv the run review handoff resolves to review.agent.md. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>The Start Implementation handoff fails to identify the target agent.</td><td class=\"ynv the start implementation handoff fails to identify the target agent. no\">No</td></tr><tr><td>Selecting Start Implementation switches chat + auto-submits via GPT-5.2 (copilot).</td><td class=\"ynv selecting start implementation switches chat + auto-submits via gpt-5.2 (copilot). yes\">Yes</td></tr></table><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Per VS Code docs, <b>handoffs.agent is the target agent identifier = the file-name stem</b> (planner example uses agent: implementation). <b>send: true</b> auto-submits the prompt; <b>model</b> takes the qualified Model (vendor) form.</div><div class=\"why\">code-review matches no file stem (No) · implementer resolves (No to fails) · send:true + GPT-5.2 (copilot) auto-submits (Yes).</div><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Example:</b> planner.agent.md has identifier <b>planner</b>. Handoff <b>agent: implementer</b> looks for implementer.agent.md (found, resolves). Handoff <b>agent: code-review</b> looks for code-review.agent.md (missing: review.agent.md does not count, identifiers match file stems, not frontmatter names).</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> VS Code custom-agent handoffs have four fields with four jobs. <b>label</b> is button text. <b>agent</b> is the target identifier, and identifiers are <b>file-name stems</b>: planner.agent.md is 'planner', implementer.agent.md is 'implementer'. This is the exam favorite: frontmatter <b>name</b> values (IMPLEMENTER, code-review) are display metadata, not identifiers. <b>prompt</b> prefills the next chat. <b>send</b> decides auto-submit (true) versus staged button (false, the default). <b>model</b> takes the qualified Model (vendor) form like GPT-5.2 (copilot). <b>Example:</b> agent: implementer resolves because the file exists; agent: code-review dangles because no code-review.agent.md exists (review.agent.md stem is 'review'). Selecting Start Implementation with send: true therefore switches to implementer and fires the prompt under GPT-5.2 immediately. When a handoff misbehaves, compare the agent value against file stems first.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (incl. handoffs)</a></li></ul>"},{"n":48,"topic":6,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Actions to validate pull requests opened by the GitHub Copilot coding agent. The workflow runs unit tests and a linter on pull request triggers, and Copilot opens draft pull requests on dedicated branches while iterating by using commits.<br />\nYou discover that when multiple Copilot sessions push updates to the same pull request branch in quick succession, multiple workflow runs execute concurrently.<br />\nYou need to enable parallel workflow executions across different pull request branches.<br />\nHow should you configure workflow-level concurrency? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image50\" src=\"images/image43.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Scope: <b>Set concurrency at the workflow level.</b><br>Expression: <b>group: ${{ github.workflow }}-${{ github.ref }}</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> A <b>concurrency group</b> serializes runs sharing a key. Keyed per branch (<b>github.ref</b>), rapid pushes to one PR queue while different PRs run in parallel. The key must scope the <b>workflow</b>, and any key containing <b>run_id</b> (unique per run) groups nothing.</div><div class=\"why\">Workflow-level per-branch group: parallel across PRs, serialized per branch.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>Job-level only</b> scopes the wrong level: pushes still overlap.</li><li><b>strategy.matrix</b> limits parallelism; it never groups runs.</li><li><b>head_ref || run_id / run_id</b>: run_id is unique, so every run is its own group.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> A <b>concurrency group</b> is a named queue: runs sharing a key execute one at a time (older ones cancel or queue). The key expression designs the queue topology. <b>github.ref</b> identifies the branch or PR ref, so grouping by workflow-plus-ref makes one queue per branch: pushes to PR-12 serialize while PR-12 and PR-13 run parallel. The group must be declared at <b>workflow level</b> to govern whole runs; job-level concurrency scopes single jobs and leaves cross-push overlap intact. Any key containing <b>run_id</b> is unique per run, producing one queue per run: mathematically no grouping at all. matrix parallelism limits fan-out, it never groups. <b>Example:</b> Copilot pushes three rapid commits to one PR branch: with group workflow-plus-ref, run 1 finishes, then 2, then 3, each testing the latest tree. With a run_id key all three overlap, waste runners, and race each other. Read the key as the queue definition.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-jobs/using-concurrency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow concurrency groups</a></li></ul>"},{"n":49,"topic":6,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub repository that contains an agent named Orchestrator.Orchestrator delegates work to the following specialized subagents:<br />\nPlanner reviews issues and creates a plan of action.<br />\nImplementer writes code based on the plan of action.<br />\nReviewer reviews the code.<br />\nYou create a new agent named Summarizer that produces a concise summary of the work performed by the other agents.<br />\nYou need to ensure that Orchestrator can invoke Summarizer as part of its workflow.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Reviewer agent, add a handoffs entry that points to the Summarizer agent.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Reviewer agent, add Summarizer to the agents list.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Orchestrator agent, add Summarizer to the tools list.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tIn the YAML frontmatter of the Orchestrator agent, add Summarizer to the agents list.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> An <b>Orchestrator</b> pattern delegates phases to subagents. Model-driven delegation reads the <b>agents array in the Orchestrator frontmatter</b>: only listed agents are callable. Handoffs drive chat buttons; the tools list holds tools, not agents.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Add Summarizer to the Orchestrator agents list</b>: listing is what makes it callable.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A/B Reviewer</b> is not the invoking agent.</li><li><b>C tools list</b>: subagents are agents, not tools.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Multi-agent orchestration has two registries beginners swap. The <b>agents array</b> in an Orchestrator frontmatter lists subagents the model may invoke programmatically during a run: this is the machine call graph. <b>Handoffs</b> list buttons a human clicks in chat to move between agents: this is the human navigation menu. The <b>tools</b> list holds tool names, and subagents are agents, not tools. The Reviewer agent profile is irrelevant because the Reviewer invokes nothing. <b>Example:</b> Orchestrator frontmatter agents: [Explorer, Modifier, Summarizer] lets the model call all three in sequence with zero clicks. Adding Summarizer to Reviewer handoffs would only paint a button nobody presses mid-run; adding it to tools would be a type error (Summarizer is not a tool). When invocation fails, check the machine registry (agents), not the human menu (handoffs).</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":50,"topic":6,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a multi-agent GitHub Actions workflow that uploads review artifacts for each run.<br />\nYou discover that some workflow run artifacts are being deleted manually.<br />\nYou need to use your organization's audit log data to identify which user deleted the artifacts.<br />\nWhich audit log search filter should you use?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\trepo:&lt;org&gt;/&lt;repo&gt;<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\taction:workflows.run<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\toperation:remove<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\taction:artifact.destroy<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>D</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The audit log is queried by field filters. Event-type filtering uses the <b>action:</b> qualifier, and artifact deletion is the <b>artifact.destroy</b> event.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>action:artifact.destroy</b>: exactly the deletion events.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A repo:</b> filters by repository, not by event.</li><li><b>B action:workflows.run</b> matches run events, not deletions.</li><li><b>C operation:remove</b> is not a valid audit filter.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The audit log is a database you query with field filters, and <b>action:</b> filters by event type. GitHub names deletion of an artifact the <b>artifact.destroy</b> event (family artifact, verb destroy), so action:artifact.destroy returns exactly those rows with actor and timestamp. The distractors query different dimensions: <b>repo:org/repo</b> slices by repository instead of by event (right database, wrong column), <b>action:workflows.run</b> matches run events rather than deletions, <b>operation:remove</b> is not valid filter syntax at all. <b>Example:</b> pasting action:artifact.destroy into the audit search instantly lists every deleted artifact org-wide this month; adding repo: narrows to one repo. Learn the two axes (action for what happened, repo/actor for where/who) and every audit question reduces to picking the right axis.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/enterprise-cloud@latest/admin/monitoring-activity-in-your-enterprise/reviewing-audit-logs-for-your-enterprise/streaming-the-audit-log-for-your-enterprise\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Audit log streaming</a></li></ul>"},{"n":51,"topic":6,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Copilot coding agent named Orchestrator that runs a multi-phase workflow by using the following subagents:<br />\nExplorer gathers context by using read-only tools.<br />\nModifier applies focused edits.<br />\nYou are adding a new agent named Summarizer that generates a concise summary after modifications are complete. Summarizer includes the following YAML frontmatter.<br />\n<img title=\"image52\" src=\"images/image45.png\"><br />\nThe Orchestrator agent lists all three agents in its agents property.<br />\nAfter adding the Summarizer agent, Orchestrator successfully runs Explorer and Modifier but fails to run Summarizer.<br />\nWhat is a possible cause of the failure?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSummarizer cannot be invoked as a subagent because disable-model-invocation is set to true.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tOrchestrator is missing a handoff entry to trigger Summarizer.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tOrchestrator cannot call Summarizer because user-invocable is set to false.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tSummarizer is missing the editing tools required to complete the workflow.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Three frontmatter flags, three meanings: <b>agents[]</b> = callable subagents, <b>user-invocable: false</b> = humans cannot @-mention it, <b>disable-model-invocation: true</b> = the model cannot call it. Explorer/Modifier run through the same agents array, so the array works: only Summarizer self-blocks.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>disable-model-invocation: true</b>: the one flag that bars model-driven calls.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B handoffs</b> drive chat buttons, not agents-array calls: Explorer/Modifier prove handoffs unnecessary.</li><li><b>C user-invocable</b> gates humans, not the model.</li><li><b>D editing tools</b>: a summary needs only fetch.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Subagent frontmatter carries three independent gates; the failure names exactly one. <b>agents[]</b> on the Orchestrator: callable subagents (Explorer, Modifier run, so this registry works). <b>user-invocable: false</b> on Summarizer: blocks humans from @-mentioning it, irrelevant to model calls. <b>disable-model-invocation: true</b> on Summarizer: blocks the model from calling it, matching the symptom precisely (siblings run, Summarizer never starts). Missing handoffs cannot matter because handoffs serve chat buttons and this run is programmatic; missing editing tools cannot matter because summarizing needs only fetch. <b>Example debugging order:</b> siblings work therefore the caller and registry are fine; the failing child differs by exactly one flag; that flag names the gate. Diff the siblings, read the differing flag, done: disable-model-invocation.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code</a></li></ul>"},{"n":52,"topic":6,"q":7,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that runs an agentic software development lifecycle (SDLC) workflow by using GitHub Actions. The workflow uses the following three executors implemented as scripts: spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer, and plan_merger.<br />\nYou need to coordinate multiple specialized agents so that analysis and risk review run in parallel, and then a final executor merges the outputs into a single plan. The orchestration pattern must fan out one request to multiple executors, and then fan in the results to a final executor.<br />\nHow should you complete the workflow definition? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image53\" src=\"images/image46.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Fan-out/fan-in: <b>needs: [spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer]</b> on plan_merger<br>Concurrency at <b>workflow level</b>, group <b>multiagent-${{ github.ref }}</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>needs</b> builds the DAG: plan_merger waits until both parallel analyzers finish (fan-out then fan-in). <b>Workflow-level concurrency</b> keyed per ref keeps branches parallel while same-branch pushes serialize.</div><div class=\"why\">needs = wait for both; per-ref workflow group = parallel branches, serial pushes.</div><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Multi-job agent pipelines are DAGs plus queues. <b>needs: [spec_analyzer, risk_reviewer]</b> on plan_merger draws two dependency arrows: the merger starts only after both analyzers finish, which is the fan-out (parallel analysis) then fan-in (single merge) shape. Without needs, all three would race and the merger would read half-written inputs. <b>Workflow-level concurrency</b> with a per-ref group (<b>multiagent-github.ref</b>) adds the queue dimension: different branches flow in parallel while rapid pushes to one branch serialize instead of overlapping. Job-level concurrency would scope the wrong level and leave cross-push races. <b>Example:</b> two PRs arrive together: four analyzer jobs run (two per PR), each PR merger waits for its own pair, and a second push to PR-7 queues behind its first run instead of corrupting it. needs orders within a run; concurrency orders runs against each other.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/using-jobs/using-concurrency\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Workflow concurrency groups</a></li></ul>"},{"n":53,"topic":7,"q":1,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  HOTSPOT<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses GitHub Copilot Chat in Microsoft Visual Studio Code. Custom agents are stored in the repository under version control.<br />\nYour team uses a multi-agent workflow where a planner agent produces an implementation plan that is then handed off to an implementation agent to make changes.<br />\nRecent prompts cause the planner agent to start editing files and running commands before the plan is approved.<br />\nYou need to configure the planner agent to meet the following requirements:<br />\nUse only read-only tools.<br />\nHand off to the implementation agent only after the plan is approved.<br />\nHow should you configure the agent? To answer, select the appropriate options in the answer area.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image55\" src=\"images/image48.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"tools: <b>[search,read,fetch]</b><br>handoff send: <b>false</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> Read-only means <b>no write power at all</b>: search, read and fetch only qualify, while edit/commit/push/merge/publish all write. And <b>send: false</b> (the default) keeps the handoff a button the human fires after approving the plan.</div><div class=\"why\">Read-only toolset + human-gated handoff.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Any list containing edit/commit/push/merge/publish grants write power.</li><li>send:true would auto-submit the handoff before approval.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Fencing a planner agent has two halves and the exhibit tests both. The <b>tools</b> half must contain only read-only capabilities: <b>search</b> (find), <b>read</b> (inspect), <b>fetch</b> (retrieve URLs). Every alternative smuggles write power: edit and commit write files, push/merge/publish mutate remotes or releases. One write tool anywhere reopens the exact misbehavior (editing before approval). The <b>handoff</b> half is governed by <b>send</b>: false (the default) stages the handoff as a button the human fires after approving the plan; true auto-submits the moment the plan text lands, before any human sees it. <b>Example:</b> with [search,read,fetch] plus send:false, the planner drafts, you read the plan, click Start Implementation, and only then does implementer touch code. Swap in publish or flip send to true and either the planner or the handoff acts pre-approval. Fence the tools, gate the transition.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://code.visualstudio.com/docs/agent-customization/custom-agents\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Custom agents in VS Code (incl. handoffs)</a></li></ul>"},{"n":54,"topic":7,"q":2,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image57\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to implement the security requirements for agent1.<br />\nWhich three actions should you perform in sequence? To answer, move the appropriate actions from the list of actions to the answer area and arrange them in the correct order.<br />\n<img title=\"image58\" src=\"images/image50.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"1. <b>Add the plan job first</b> (uploads plan output as artifact)<br>2. <b>Create the plan-review environment with SG_Review as required reviewers</b><br>3. <b>Add the execute job with needs: [plan] and environment: plan-review</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The security demands: <b>only SG_Review approves plan outputs</b>, execution waits for that approval. GitHub <b>environments</b> gate jobs behind named required reviewers; <b>needs</b> orders the jobs. Order is load-bearing: produce, gate, then execute.</div><div class=\"why\">Produce the plan, gate it behind SG_Review, then execute the approved plan.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li>Auto-advance on green skips the required approval.</li><li>A 2-approval main-branch ruleset gates pushes, not plan outputs.</li><li>SG_Dev as reviewers violates SG_Review-only approval.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Human-gated agent execution is a three-act pipeline and order is load-bearing. <b>Act 1, the plan job</b> runs first and uploads the plan as a workflow artifact: the thing to be approved must exist as a reviewable object. <b>Act 2, the plan-review environment</b> with <b>SG_Review as required reviewers</b> is the lock: GitHub environments pause jobs until named humans approve, and only SG_Review satisfies 'only SG_Review approves plan outputs'. <b>Act 3, the execute job</b> declares <b>needs: [plan]</b> (runs after the plan) and <b>environment: plan-review</b> (waits behind the lock). The distractors each break one act: auto-advance removes the lock, a main-branch 2-approval ruleset guards pushes instead of plan outputs, SG_Dev reviewers violate the only-SG_Review rule. <b>Example:</b> agent drafts a migration plan, SG_Review gets a pending-deployment notice, a senior approves, and only then does execute run the migration. Produce, gate, execute: in that order, always.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/managing-workflow-runs-and-deployments/managing-deployments/managing-environments-for-deployment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Environments + required reviewers</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"},{"n":55,"topic":7,"q":3,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Existing Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nThe GitHub environment contains the following:<br />\nThree repositories named product-api, billing-service, and infra-terraform.<br />\nBranch protection on the main branch in all repositories that requires at least one pull request review before merging<br />\nGitHub Actions runners used across all workflows<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Dev that contains developers<br />\nA GitHub team named SG_Review that contains senior engineers and a security team<br />\nA .github/copilot-instructions.md file that includes general coding conventions for all features<br />\nAgent environment<br />\nThe product-api repository uses a GitHub Copilot coding agent named agent1 that has the following configurations:<br />\nNo custom agent profile is defined.<br />\nA Model Context Protocol (MCP) server named MCP1 is deployed to https://mcp.litwareinc.internal and provides access to internal ticketing and deployment APIs. MCP1 requires an API key for authentication.<br />\nA second Copilot coding agent named agent2 handles changes in infra-terraform and runs in parallel with agent1 when both agents have open assigned issues.<br />\nCopilot memory is NOT enabled for the organization.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nLitware identifies the following issues:<br />\nDuring two recent sessions, agent1 accessed files in billing-service, which is outside the agent's intended scope.<br />\nagent1 makes code changes immediately after receiving a task.<br />\nA developer named Ben, who is on the SG_Dev team, reports that agent1 completed a session with a successful status and opened a pull request, but the pull request contains no file changes. Other developers report this intermittently as well.<br />\nBoth agent1 and agent2 modified shared/config.yaml in a parallel test run, generating conflicting outputs.<br />\nagent1 consistently uses raw try-catch blocks for error handling, which violates the defined implementation guidelines of SG_Dev.<br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nLitware plans to make the following changes:<br />\nEnsure that agent1 can access all the tools in the environment.<br />\nProvide product-api with specific instructions to agent1 without affecting Copilot Chat or Copilot code review.<br />\nConfigure MCP1 as a tool for agent1 by modifying the product-api repository MCP configuration.<br />\nEnsure that Copilot retains details that it has learned and uses that knowledge for future work. This must be applied to all licensed members of the organization.<br />\nImplementation guidelines<br />\nThe development team at Litware identifies the following implementation guidelines:<br />\nAgent workflows must be able to run in parallel.<br />\nApplication error handling must use the repository ErrorHandler class.<br />\nagent1 and agent2 must run on isolated branches during parallel execution. File-level conflicts must be detected before merges, and both agents must be able to run concurrently.<br />\nSecurity requirements<br />\nLitware identifies the following security requirements:<br />\nOnly the members of SG_Review must be able to approve agent1 plan outputs.<br />\nAll API keys must be stored and accessed securely.<br />\nThe developers must NOT be able to self-approve.<br />\nAgent configuration<br />\n<img title=\"image60\" src=\"images/image10.png\"><br />\nYou need to resolve the scoping issue associated to agent1.<br />\nWhat should you do?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tTo the profile of agent1, add a custom instruction specifying that the agent must NOT access billing-service or infra-terraform.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tAdd a permissions block to the agent1 workflow in product-api.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a fine-grained personal access token (PAT) scoped to product-api and store the PAT as a GitHub Actions secret for agent1 to use.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tCreate a ruleset for billing-service and infra-terraform that blocks push access from the github-actions bot account.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The agent touched repos outside its scope because its <b>credential</b> allowed it. A <b>fine-grained PAT</b> can be limited to one repository: scoped to product-api, billing-service and infra-terraform become unreachable. Prose instructions are unenforceable, and rulesets only block pushes.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Scoped PAT</b>: fix the scope at the credential layer.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A prose instruction</b> (do not access X) is unenforced: agents can ignore it.</li><li><b>B permissions block</b> scopes GITHUB_TOKEN, not the agent session credential.</li><li><b>D bot-push ruleset</b> blocks only pushes by one identity: reads continue, and the agent may not even push as the bot.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Agents go where their credentials allow, not where prose tells them. agent1 read billing-service because the credential it runs with reaches beyond product-api. Fixes divide into enforced versus wished. A <b>fine-grained PAT scoped to product-api</b> is enforced: API calls to other repos fail at authentication, so the scoping issue disappears at the credential layer regardless of agent behavior. A prose instruction ('never touch billing-service') is wished: models can and do ignore guidance under task pressure. A <b>workflow permissions block</b> scopes GITHUB_TOKEN, a different credential than the agent session uses. A <b>bot-push ruleset</b> on the other repos blocks one identity pushes only: reads continue, and the agent may not push as that bot anyway. <b>Example:</b> with the scoped PAT, agent1 asking for a billing-service file gets a 404 from the API: the failure is structural, auditable, and permanent. Scope the credential, not the conversation.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/authentication/keeping-your-account-and-data-secure/managing-your-personal-access-tokens\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fine-grained personal access tokens</a></li><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/security-for-github-actions/security-guides/automatic-token-authentication\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GITHUB_TOKEN permissions</a></li></ul>"},{"n":56,"topic":7,"q":4,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  Case study<br><br>Overview<br />\nContoso Ltd. is a software development company located in the United States.<br />\nExisting Environment<br />\nGitHub Environment<br />\nContoso uses GitHub Enterprise and assigns GitHub Copilot Pro+ licenses to its developers. The developers use Microsoft Visual Studio Code as their IDE.<br />\nContoso has a customer portal. The code for the portal is stored in a GitHub repository named repo1 that contains the following:<br />\nA custom agent named agent1 that includes instructions to review specs related to best practices<br />\nA custom instruction file named validate-instructions.md that is used to validate tone of voice and applies to all .md and .txt files<br />\nA custom instruction file named codereview.instructions.md that is used by the Copilot coding agent but is excluded for use by the Copilot code review<br />\nrepo1 has the following structure:<br />\nThe front-end is stored in the /frontend folder.<br />\nThe API logic is stored in the /api folder.<br />\nContoso has a second repository named repo2 that contains a legacy .NET application named App1 built by using .NET 6. repo2 has a multi-agent workflow for modernization tasks.<br />\nContoso enables the Model Context Protocol (MCP) registry and allows the Microsoft Learn MCP Server. Every developer must configure their own connection to the Learn MCP Server.<br />\nProblem Statements<br />\nThe developers working in repo1 report that the Microsoft Learn documentation is NOT being retrieved when they attempt to validate a design by using agent1.<br />\nThe testing team at Contoso identifies that the customer portal uses inconsistent UI styles, which leads to customer confusion and branding issues. The UI inconsistencies stem from variations in the folder structure.<br />\n<br />\nAgent Logs<br />\nYou have the following logs for the multi-agent workflow used in repo2.<br />\n<img title=\"image61\" src=\"images/image1.png\"><br />\nRequirements<br />\nPlanned Changes<br />\nContoso plans to have all agents and developers in repo1 use the Microsoft Learn MCP to ensure that reviews are validated by using the appropriate documentation. This must be implemented centrally.<br />\nContoso plans to leverage AI-powered coding agents to implement new portal features and pages.<br />\nTechnical Requirements<br />\nApp1 must be upgraded to .NET 10. A previous upgrade attempt was started by using the Copilot modernization agent, but the attempt was never finalized.<br />\nYou plan to retry the upgrade. You must first analyze App1 by using AI, and then generate a report that contains breaking changes and deprecated patterns before retrying the upgrade.<br />\nAll AI-generated code for UI styling must adhere to a predefined folder structure.<br />\nThe architects at Contoso need help building implementation plans for repo1. The company wants to implement a new agent named agent2 to analyze the code base and the code requirements, and then respond with a detailed plan. The agent must NOT be able to edit files or run local commands.<br />\nThe developers must be able to delegate work to the Copilot coding agent by assigning issues to the agent.<br />\nWhile upgrading App1, the agent identifies 47 issues, including a security vulnerability, and 46 API incompatibilities across different projects.<br />\nWhich two actions are unsafe to delegate to the agent and require human involvement? Each correct answer presents a complete solution.<br><br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tValidate the assessment.md file for accuracy.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tGenerate the assessment.md file.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tApprove all Git commits.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tReview the plan.md file for dependencies.<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>E.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tValidate whether the tasks.md file exists.<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>A, C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> The agent surfaced 47 issues including a <b>security vulnerability</b>. Generating reports and analyzing dependencies is exactly agent work; but <b>judging correctness of security-relevant findings</b> and <b>approving commits</b> (the merge gate) require accountable human judgment.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Validate assessment + approve commits</b>: the two human gates.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>B generating</b> the assessment is core agent work.</li><li><b>D reviewing plan dependencies</b> is delegable analysis.</li><li><b>E checking tasks.md exists</b> is a trivial filesystem check.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> Delegation safety tracks accountability, not difficulty. Agents excel at <b>generating</b> (assessment.md drafts), <b>analyzing</b> (dependency review of plan.md) and <b>checking</b> (does tasks.md exist): all reversible, all verifiable. Two actions are unsafe because no agent self-check substitutes for them. <b>Validating assessment accuracy</b> when 47 findings include a security vulnerability means judging truth with breach consequences: a wrong 'all clear' ships a vulnerability. <b>Approving Git commits</b> is the merge gate: approval publishes code to the shared tree. Both need an accountable human. <b>Example:</b> the agent drafts the assessment (safe), flags a possible auth bypass (analysis, safe), you verify the bypass is real (human judgment, unsafe to delegate), then you approve the fix commits (human gate). Generate freely, judge and release personally.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-app-modernization/concepts\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">App modernization: assessment, plan, tasks.md</a></li><li><a href=\"https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/porting/github-copilot-upgrade/overview\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot upgrade overview</a></li></ul>"},{"n":57,"topic":7,"q":5,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  DRAG DROP<br />\nYou have a GitHub repository that uses the GitHub Copilot CLI to run autonomous tasks.<br />\nYou need to validate each generated command before it runs. Any commands that attempt to modify paths outside the repository must be blocked.<br />\nHow should you complete the YAML? To answer, drag the appropriate values to the correct targets. Each value may be used once, more than once, or not at all. You may need to drag the split bar between panes or scroll to view content.<br />\n<br />\n<img title=\"image62\" src=\"images/image52.png\"><br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"event: <b>pre-exec</b><br>config: <b>.copilot/hooks.yaml</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> This hook format (version/hooks/cli) guards generated shell commands: the script reads <b></b> and exits nonzero to veto. Vetoing must happen <b>before execution</b>, and the CLI harness reads <b>.copilot/hooks.yaml</b>, never an Actions workflow path.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>pre-exec + .copilot/hooks.yaml</b>: validate before run, in the file the harness reads.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>post-exec</b> runs after the command: blocking there is too late.</li><li><b>.github/workflows/hooks.yaml</b> is a CI workflow path the CLI harness never reads.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> The Copilot CLI agent harness validates generated shell commands through a guardrail file in its own format: <b>.copilot/hooks.yaml</b> with version, hooks (name, event, language, run script) and a cli section pointing at it. The script receives the candidate command in <b></b> and vetoes with nonzero exit: here a grep rejects absolute paths and parent traversal (..), blocking every path escape outside the repo. Two choices are load-bearing. <b>event: pre-exec</b> runs validation before execution: post-exec would judge damage already done. The <b>config path</b> must be the harness file: a same-shaped YAML under .github/workflows/ is a CI workflow the CLI never reads. <b>Example:</b> the agent emits 'cat ../../etc/passwd': pre-exec catches the traversal, prints the guardrail message, exits 1, command never runs. Validate before, in the file the harness reads.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/copilot-cli\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot CLI</a></li></ul>"},{"n":58,"topic":7,"q":6,"html":"<div class=\"question-body mt-3 pt-3 border-top\" data-id=\"825909\">\r\n                <p class=\"card-text\">\r\n                  You have a GitHub Enterprise Cloud Organization that uses GitHub Actions for CI/CD.<br />\nYou plan to enable an agent to update and deploy workflows that access production environment secrets.<br />\nYou need to select an autonomy level for a compliance-sensitive workflow.<br />\nWhich autonomy level should you select?<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>A.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tread-only analysis with no ability to trigger workflows<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>B.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tautonomous execution limited to non-production environments<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>C.\r\n\t\t\t\t\thuman-in-the-loop with required manual approvals for production<br>\r\n\t\t\t\t  <br>D.\r\n\t\t\t\t\tfully autonomous execution without approvals<br>\r\n                </p>","ans":"Correct answer: <b>C</b><div class=\"bg\"><b>New to this?</b> <b>Autonomy levels</b> trade speed for control. Compliance-sensitive production deploys with secrets demand a human release gate: the agent may prepare everything, but only <b>required manual approvals</b> let it touch production.</div><div class=\"why\"><b>Human-in-the-loop + manual production approvals</b>: preparation automated, release human.</div><span class=\"whead\">Why the others are wrong:</span><ul><li><b>A read-only</b> cannot deploy at all.</li><li><b>B non-prod-only</b> excludes the required production target.</li><li><b>D fully autonomous</b> with secrets and production access violates compliance.</li></ul><div class=\"lesson\"><b>Learn it properly:</b> <b>Autonomy levels</b> trade speed against control on a fixed ladder. Read-only analysis cannot change anything (safe, useless for deploys). Non-production autonomy ships to staging freely (fast feedback, zero prod risk). <b>Human-in-the-loop with required manual approvals</b> lets the agent prepare everything but blocks production effects until named humans approve (the compliance posture: automation proposes, humans dispose). Fully autonomous execution removes every gate (fastest, unacceptable where secrets and production meet compliance). The requirement (agent updates and deploys workflows touching production secrets, compliance-sensitive) pins exactly one rung. <b>Example:</b> the agent drafts the workflow change and stages it to the production environment, which pauses showing 'waiting for review': a release manager approves after checking the diff, and only then do secrets flow. Read the requirement as constraints (production? secrets? compliance?), then pick the lowest rung satisfying all three.</div><span class=\"whead\">Learn more:</span><ul class=\"learn\"><li><a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/copilot/concepts/agents/coding-agent\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Copilot coding agent</a></li></ul>"}];
